insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-grammar-1', 'course-grammar', 'تطابق الفعل مع الفاعل والأزمنة', 0, '[{"type":"youtube","url":"https://www.engvid.com/subject-verb-agreement/","title":"Singular or Plural? Subject-Verb Agreement in English Grammar","provider":"engVid","youtubeId":"wq7RYyhwfBA"},{"type":"youtube","url":"https://www.engvid.com/learn-english-tenses-review/","title":"Learn English Tenses: Review of ALL 12 TENSES in English","provider":"engVid","youtubeId":"aUJO_0phvbw"}]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
الفعل يتبع الفاعل في العدد (مفرد/جمع)، لا الاسم الأقرب إليه في الجملة. العبارات "Neither of"، "Each of"، و"The number of" تُعامل دائمًا كفاعل مفرد، بينما "A number of" تكون جمعًا.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• Neither/Each/The number of + اسم جمع + فعل مفرد.
• A number of + اسم جمع + فعل جمع.
• الماضي التام = had + اسم المفعول، لحدث سبق حدثًا ماضيًا آخر.

⚠️ فخ STEP:
لا تنخدع بالاسم الواقع مباشرة قبل الفعل. في "The number of students who cheat (is/are)" الفعل يتبع the number وليس students.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — حدد الفاعل قبل اختيار الفعل.
• اليوم 3 — أجب عن سؤال تطابق مع عبارة The number of.
• اليوم 7 — حل أسئلة التطابق المركّبة في الاختبار التجريبي.
• اليوم 14 — راجع قاعدة الماضي التام بمثال من نصوص القراءة.

🧭 تكنيكة اجدا (AGDA) للجرامر:
• (أ) اقرأ الاختيارات أولاً لتحديد القاعدة المستهدفة (لا تبدأ بالسؤال).
• (ج) خمّن القاعدة النحوية: أزمنة؟ مقارنة؟ حروف جر؟ مبني للمجهول؟ شرطية؟...
• (د) حدد الكلمات الدلالية في الجملة (مفاتيح زمنية أو تركيبية).
• (ا) استبعد الخيارات غير المتوافقة نحواً أو زمناً.
• ⏱️ قاعدة الـ45 ثانية: إن لم تصل للإجابة خلال 45 ثانية خمّن وضع إجابة وانتقل فوراً، وحلّ بالترتيب دون قفز.', array['q-grammar-1','q-grammar-2','q-grammar-7']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-reading-1', 'course-reading', 'قراءة الفكرة الرئيسية والتفاصيل', 3, '[]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
الفكرة الرئيسية تظهر غالبًا في الجملة الأولى من الفقرة، والخلاصة في الأخيرة؛ التفاصيل الداعمة تُبنى حولهما.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• اقرأ أول وأخير جملة من كل فقرة قبل التفاصيل.
• لسؤال تفصيلي: ابحث عن الكلمات المفتاحية في السؤال داخل النص حرفيًا.

⚠️ فخ STEP:
لا تختر خيارًا يبدو "مألوفًا" لكنه غير مذكور في النص — كل خيار خاطئ يتعارض عادة مع جملة واحدة واضحة.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — طبّق (أول+أخير) على فقرة قصيرة.
• اليوم 3 — حل سؤال تفصيلي بالبحث عن الكلمات المفتاحية.
• اليوم 7 — ميّز الفكرة الرئيسية عن التفصيل في اختبار.
• اليوم 14 — راجع تقنية المسح الضوئي قبل الاختبار.

🧭 تكنيكة اسما (ASMA) للقراءة:
• (أ) أول سطر: اقرأ أول سطر من كل فقرة لتخمين الموضوع العام.
• (س) السؤال: اقرأ السؤال وحدد الكلمات الدلالية (Keywords).
• (م) مكان الإجابة: ابحث بعينيك عن الكلمات الدلالية واقرأ جملتها بتركيز.
• (ا) استبعد الاختيارات الخاطئة والبعيدة لتأكيد الصحيح.
• ابدأ بأسئلة المعنى والضمائر أولاً، وابحث عن المرادفات (Synonyms).
• ممنوع: قراءة القطعة بأسلوب الجرائد، أو الحفظ/المعلومات العامة، أو ترك أي سؤال فارغ.', array['q-reading-1','q-reading-2']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-listening-1', 'course-listening', 'متابعة المحادثات القصيرة', 2, '[]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
المتحدث الثاني غالبًا يصحّح أو يضيف تفصيلًا حاسمًا بعد كلمات انتقالية مثل No أو Actually.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• راقب بداية رد المتحدث الثاني: No / Actually = تصحيح.
• المعلومة الصحيحة تأتي بعد التصحيح وليس قبله.

⚠️ فخ STEP:
الخيارات الخاطئة تكرر المعلومة الأولى قبل كلمة No؛ الإجابة الصحيحة هي ما ورد بعد التصحيح.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — علّم كلمات التصحيح في حوار قصير.
• اليوم 3 — أجب عن سؤال تصحيح في محادثة.
• اليوم 7 — حل أسئلة المحادثات القصيرة في اختبار.
• اليوم 14 — راجع إشارات التصحيح قبل الاختبار.', array['q-listening-1','q-listening-4']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-writingAnalysis-1', 'course-writingAnalysis', 'اكتشاف الخطأ النحوي بسرعة', 0, '[]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
ترتيب الفحص: تطابق الفعل مع الفاعل أولًا (الخطأ الأكثر تكرارًا)، ثم حروف الجر، وأخيرًا العبارات الافتتاحية المعلّقة (dangling modifiers).

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• الخطوة 1: هل الفعل متطابق مع الفاعل؟
• الخطوة 2: هل حرف الجر صحيح بعد الكلمة التي تسبقه؟
• الخطوة 3: هل العبارة الافتتاحية تصف الفاعل الذي يليها مباشرة؟

⚠️ فخ STEP:
أخطاء حروف الجر تقع غالبًا في نهاية الجملة، ولا تظهر إن ركزت على التوافق فقط — أكمل الترتيب كاملًا.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — طبّق الترتيب على 3 جمل خاطئة.
• اليوم 3 — اكتشف خطأ حرف جر وحده.
• اليوم 7 — حل أسئلة التحليل الكتابي في اختبار.
• اليوم 14 — راجع أنواع الأخطاء الأربعة قبل الاختبار.', array['q-writing-1','q-writing-3']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-vocabulary-1', 'course-vocabulary', 'التكرار المتباعد لحفظ المفردات', 0, '[]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
التكرار المتباعد (بعد يوم، ثم 3 أيام، ثم أسبوع) يثبّت الكلمة في الذاكرة طويلة المدى أفضل من 10 مراجعات في يوم واحد.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• اليوم 1: تعلم الكلمة بجملة.
• اليوم 3: استرجعها دون النظر.
• اليوم 7: استخدمها في جملة جديدة.

⚠️ فخ STEP:
المراجعة المكثفة في جلسة واحدة تعطي إحساسًا خادعًا بالحفظ؛ الفجوات بين المراجعات هي ما يثبّت الحفظ فعلًا.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — احفظ 10 كلمات بجمل.
• اليوم 3 — استرجع القائمة دون النظر.
• اليوم 7 — راجع الكلمات المنسية فقط.
• اليوم 14 — راجع البطاقات كلها قبل الاختبار.', array[]::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-tips-1', 'course-tips', 'عادات صغيرة، فرق كبير', 0, '[]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
قراءة الخيارات الأربعة كاملة قبل الاختيار تمنع الوقوع في الخيار الأول الخادع.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• اقرأ الخيارات الأربعة دائمًا حتى لو بدا الأول صحيحًا.
• علّم الأسئلة المؤكدة الإجابة بعلامة واضحة.

⚠️ فخ STEP:
الخيار الأول الصحيح ظاهريًا غالبًا ناقص تفصيل موجود في خيار لاحق؛ لا توقف القراءة عند الأول.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — طبّق قراءة الأربعة على اختبار قصير.
• اليوم 3 — درّب تعليم الأسئلة المؤجلة.
• اليوم 7 — طبّق الاستراتيجية في اختبار كامل.
• اليوم 14 — راجع نمطها قبل الاختبار الرسمي.', array[]::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-strategies-1', 'course-strategies', 'توزيع 150 دقيقة على 100 سؤال', 0, '[]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
توزيع 150 دقيقة على 100 سؤال: القراءة والنحو يحتاجان وقتًا أطول لكل سؤال من الاستماع، والحد الآمن 90 ثانية للسؤال الواحد.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• قسم القراءة/النحو: حتى 90 ثانية لكل سؤال.
• قسم الاستماع: التزم بسرعة التسجيل لا بأكثر.
• لا تتجاوز 90 ثانية في سؤال نحو أو تحليل كتابي.

⚠️ فخ STEP:
حساب الوقت الخاطئ: إذا أمضيت دقيقتين في سؤال واحد، فأنت تسحب وقت سؤالين لاحقين — توقف عند الحد.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — احسب وقتك المثالي لكل قسم.
• اليوم 3 — طبّق الحد الزمني في اختبار قصير.
• اليوم 7 — حل اختبار كامل بالمؤقت.
• اليوم 14 — راجع الجدول الزمني قبل الاختبار.', array[]::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-revision-1', 'course-revision', 'مراجعة أسبوعية شاملة', 0, '[]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
المراجعة الأسبوعية بأسئلة مختارة من جميع الأقسام تثبّت ما تعلمته وتكشف الفجوات.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• حل أسئلة كل قسم ثم راجع الخطأ مباشرة.
• علّم الأنماط التي أخطأت فيها لتكررها لاحقًا.

⚠️ فخ STEP:
مراجعة الأسئلة التي أجبتها صحيحًا فقط تضيع الوقت؛ ركّز على أخطائك.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — حل أسئلة المراجعة.
• اليوم 3 — أعد حل الأخطاء دون النظر للإجابات.
• اليوم 7 — حل اختبار قصير للقياس.
• اليوم 14 — راجع قائمة الأخطاء المتكررة.', array['q-grammar-3','q-reading-4','q-listening-2','q-writing-2']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-grammar-2', 'course-grammar', 'الصوت المبني للمجهول والاستنتاج بالأفعال الناقصة', 8, '[{"type":"youtube","url":"https://www.engvid.com/learn-english-grammar-active-passive/","title":"Learn English Grammar: What''s the difference between ACTIVE & PASSIVE?","provider":"engVid","youtubeId":"fDnXWxXZekw"},{"type":"youtube","url":"https://www.engvid.com/english-grammar-modal-verbs-of-certainty/","title":"English Grammar: Modal Verbs of Certainty – MIGHT, MAY, MUST, CAN","provider":"engVid","youtubeId":"wdhps_l-5d8"}]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
المبني للمجهول يُستخدم عندما يكون الفاعل غير معروف أو غير مهم؛ والاستنتاج المنطقي يستخدم must للأدلة القوية وmight للاحتمال الضعيف.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• المجهول = be بالزمن المطلوب + اسم المفعول (will be announced / was published).
• must have + اسم المفعول = استنتاج قوي عن الماضي (He must have left).
• might have + اسم المفعول = احتمال أضعف.
• في الكلام المنقول الماضي: will ← would.

⚠️ فخ STEP:
عندما يرد "will be announced" لا تختار "will announce"؛ غياب الفاعل هو المؤشر الحاسم. وكلمة must في جملة الماضي تتحول إلى must have done وليس must did.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — تحويل جملة معلومة إلى مجهولة.
• اليوم 3 — ميّز must/might من قوة الدليل.
• اليوم 7 — حل أسئلة الكلام المنقول في الاختبار.
• اليوم 14 — أعد صياغة فقرة قصيرة كاملة بالمبني للمجهول.

🧭 تكنيكة اجدا (AGDA) للجرامر:
• (أ) اقرأ الاختيارات أولاً لتحديد القاعدة المستهدفة (لا تبدأ بالسؤال).
• (ج) خمّن القاعدة النحوية: أزمنة؟ مقارنة؟ حروف جر؟ مبني للمجهول؟ شرطية؟...
• (د) حدد الكلمات الدلالية في الجملة (مفاتيح زمنية أو تركيبية).
• (ا) استبعد الخيارات غير المتوافقة نحواً أو زمناً.
• ⏱️ قاعدة الـ45 ثانية: إن لم تصل للإجابة خلال 45 ثانية خمّن وضع إجابة وانتقل فوراً، وحلّ بالترتيب دون قفز.', array['q-grammar-10','q-grammar-11','q-grammar-14']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-reading-2', 'course-reading', 'استخراج المعلومة الضمنية والصريحة', 4, '[]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
هناك نوعان من أسئلة الفهم: صريحة (مذكورة حرفيًا) وضمنية (تُستنتج من مجموع الجمل)، وكل نوع له خطة إجابة مختلفة.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• سؤال صريح: اسأل "أين هذه الجملة بالضبط في النص؟".
• سؤال استنتاجي: اسأل "ما الذي يلمّح إليه الكاتب دون قوله مباشرة؟".

⚠️ فخ STEP:
الخيار الصحيح للسؤال الاستنتاجي لا يظهر كجملة حرفية في النص؛ إن رأيته حرفيًا فغالبًا يكون خاطئًا (إجابة سؤال صريح وليس ضمنيًا).

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — صنّف 3 أسئلة إلى صريحة/ضمنية.
• اليوم 3 — أجب عن سؤال ضمني بعيدًا عن النص الحرفي.
• اليوم 7 — حل أسئلة inference في اختبار تجريبي.
• اليوم 14 — راجع مؤشرات الاستدلال (imply, suggest, infer).

🧭 تكنيكة اسما (ASMA) للقراءة:
• (أ) أول سطر: اقرأ أول سطر من كل فقرة لتخمين الموضوع العام.
• (س) السؤال: اقرأ السؤال وحدد الكلمات الدلالية (Keywords).
• (م) مكان الإجابة: ابحث بعينيك عن الكلمات الدلالية واقرأ جملتها بتركيز.
• (ا) استبعد الاختيارات الخاطئة والبعيدة لتأكيد الصحيح.
• ابدأ بأسئلة المعنى والضمائر أولاً، وابحث عن المرادفات (Synonyms).
• ممنوع: قراءة القطعة بأسلوب الجرائد، أو الحفظ/المعلومات العامة، أو ترك أي سؤال فارغ.', array['q-reading-7','q-reading-10']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-listening-2', 'course-listening', 'محادثات في سياقات رسمية', 3, '[]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
في المحادثات الرسمية محور السؤال هو التفاصيل الرقمية (الوقت/العدد) وسبب اختيار المتحدث.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• سمّع الرقم المطلوب (وقت، مبلغ، عدد) — هو غالبًا الإجابة.
• اسمع السبب بعد عبارات like because / since / that''s why.

⚠️ فخ STEP:
قد يُذكر أكثر من رقم في الحوار؛ السؤال يطلب الرقم النهائي المختار، لا كل الأرقام المذكورة.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — التقط الأرقام من حوار رسمي.
• اليوم 3 — أجب عن سؤال السبب النهائي.
• اليوم 7 — حل أسئلة الحجوزات في اختبار.
• اليوم 14 — راجع تعابير التفضيل (works better, prefer).', array['q-listening-7','q-listening-10']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-writingAnalysis-2', 'course-writingAnalysis', 'أخطاء التوازي وحالة الضمير', 1, '[]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
عند قائمة أفعال معطوفة بـ and يجب أن تكون جميع عناصرها بنفس الصيغة النحوية (توازي)، وبعد حروف الجر مثل between تُستخدم ضمائر المفعول لا ضمائر الفاعل.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• قائمة: فعل + فعل + فعل بنفس الصيغة (swimming, running, cycling).
• after حرف جر: ضمير مفعول (between you and me).

⚠️ فخ STEP:
الخيار الخاطئ يخالف توازيًا في قائمة من ثلاثة عناصر، أو يضع I بدل me بعد between.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — اكتشف العنصر المكسور في قائمة غير متوازية.
• اليوم 3 — صحّح جملة بين you and I.
• اليوم 7 — حل أسئلة التوازي في اختبار تجريبي.
• اليوم 14 — راجع قوائم الأفعال المتوازية قبل الاختبار.', array['q-writing-5','q-writing-7']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-vocabulary-2', 'course-vocabulary', 'مفردات أكاديمية شائعة في القراءة', 1, '[]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
كلمات مثل infrastructure وcurriculum وsustained تتكرر في نصوص القراءة الأكاديمية، وحفظها داخل جملة كاملة أثبت من حفظها منفردة.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• احفظ الكلمة في جملة سياقية (The city upgraded its infrastructure).
• اربط الكلمة بموضوعها (curriculum ← التعليم).

⚠️ فخ STEP:
الكلمة المحفوظة بمعزى واحد تفشل أمام سؤال سياق مختلف؛ تعرّف على الكلمة في مواقف متعددة.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — احفظ 5 كلمات أكاديمية بجمل.
• اليوم 3 — ضعها في جمل جديدة من كتابة يدك.
• اليوم 7 — حل سؤال مفردات في سياق قراءة.
• اليوم 14 — راجع قائمة المفردات الأكاديمية.', array[]::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-tips-2', 'course-tips', 'قبل الاختبار بيوم', 1, '[]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
في آخر يوم: مراجعة الأخطاء المتكررة أهم من مذاكرة مواد جديدة، والنوم الكافي أداة أداء حقيقية.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• راجع أخطاء اختباراتك السابقة فقط.
• تأكد من مكان الاختبار والوقت مسبقًا.

⚠️ فخ STEP:
مذاكرة شيء جديد في آخر ليلة تُربك معلوماتك الراسخة وتقلل ثقتك؛ التركيز على المراجعة أخف وأفيد.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — اجمع أخطاءك المتكررة في قائمة.
• اليوم 3 — راجع القائمة فقط.
• اليوم 7 — حل اختبار سريع لقياس التقدم.
• اليوم 14 — طبّق روتين الليلة السابقة.', array[]::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-strategies-2', 'course-strategies', 'متى تتجاوز سؤالاً صعبًا', 1, '[]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
السؤال الذي يستغرق أكثر من دقيقتين دون تقدّم يجب تأجيله؛ العودة إليه لاحقًا غالبًا تنجح بمنظور أوضح.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• تجاوز: علّم السؤال وانتقل فورًا بعد دقيقتين.
• عُد في وقت المراجعة النهائي.

⚠️ فخ STEP:
الإصرار على سؤال صعب يخسر وقت سؤالين أسهل؛ التأجيل ليس هروبًا بل إدارة وقت ذكية.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — درّب التجاوز على سؤال عالق.
• اليوم 3 — راجع طريقة العودة المؤجلة.
• اليوم 7 — طبّقها في اختبار كامل.
• اليوم 14 — راجع قاعدة الدقيقتين قبل الاختبار.', array[]::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-revision-2', 'course-revision', 'مراجعة أسبوعية ٢', 1, '[]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
جولة ثانية من الأسئلة المختارة تثبّت المعلومات في الذاكرة طويلة المدى عبر التكرار المتباعد.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• حل الجولة الثانية دون الرجوع لملاحظاتك.
• قارن أداءك بالجولة الأولى.

⚠️ فخ STEP:
الأخطاء المتكررة في الجولتين تشير إلى فجوة حقيقية؛ ادرسها من الدرس المختص قبل المتابعة.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — حل الجولة الثانية.
• اليوم 3 — راجع أخطاء الجولتين.
• اليوم 7 — حل أسئلة الأنماط الضعيفة.
• اليوم 14 — راجع تقدمك عبر الجولات.', array['q-grammar-12','q-reading-8','q-listening-8','q-writing-6']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-grammar-3', 'course-grammar', 'تراكيب متقدمة: السببية والانقلاب والجمل الاسمية', 13, '[{"type":"youtube","url":"https://www.engvid.com/english-grammar-causative-verbs/","title":"English Grammar: Causative Verbs: Make, Have, Let, Get, Help","provider":"engVid","youtubeId":"ZyfLxDTlsOQ"},{"type":"youtube","url":"https://www.engvid.com/english-grammar-inversion/","title":"English Grammar – Inversion: “Had I known…”, “Should you need…”","provider":"engVid","youtubeId":"4K0npnSYjJE"}]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
التركيب السببي (causative) يعبّر عن فعل يقوم به شخص آخر نيابة عنك؛ والانقلاب (inversion) يقلب ترتيب الجملة بعد ظروف سلبية في بدايتها.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• have/get + شيء + اسم المفعول (have my car repaired).
• Rarely/Seldom/Hardly + فعل مساعد + فاعل (Rarely do we see).
• whether يبدأ جملة اسمية في موضع الفاعل، وif لا.

⚠️ فخ STEP:
لا تقل have my car repaired بـ "have repaired my car" — الفاعل لا يقوم بالعمل. وعندما تبدأ الجملة بـ Rarely، لا تنسَ الفعل المساعد قبل الفاعل.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — كوّن جملتين سببيتين.
• اليوم 3 — أعد صياغة جملة تبدأ بـ Rarely مع انقلاب.
• اليوم 7 — حل سؤال سببية في القسم الكتابي.
• اليوم 14 — اكتب 3 جمل اسمية بـ whether وشرح فرقها عن if.

🧭 تكنيكة اجدا (AGDA) للجرامر:
• (أ) اقرأ الاختيارات أولاً لتحديد القاعدة المستهدفة (لا تبدأ بالسؤال).
• (ج) خمّن القاعدة النحوية: أزمنة؟ مقارنة؟ حروف جر؟ مبني للمجهول؟ شرطية؟...
• (د) حدد الكلمات الدلالية في الجملة (مفاتيح زمنية أو تركيبية).
• (ا) استبعد الخيارات غير المتوافقة نحواً أو زمناً.
• ⏱️ قاعدة الـ45 ثانية: إن لم تصل للإجابة خلال 45 ثانية خمّن وضع إجابة وانتقل فوراً، وحلّ بالترتيب دون قفز.', array['q-grammar-17','q-grammar-18','q-grammar-20']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-reading-3', 'course-reading', 'قراءة نصوص الرأي والجدل', 5, '[]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
نصوص الجدل تعرض رأي طرفين على الأقل؛ والسؤال يختبر قدرتك على عدم الخلط بين حجة كل طرف.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• حدد المؤيدين والمعارضين كمجموعتين منفصلتين.
• لكل حجة، اسأل: "من صاحبها؟" قبل أن تجيب.

⚠️ فخ STEP:
الخيارات الخاطئة تخلط عادة حجة المؤيد داخل سؤال عن المعارض. انتبه لعبارات النسب (supporters argue, critics claim).

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — حدد رأي الطرفين في نص جدلي قصير.
• اليوم 3 — أجب عن سؤال موجه لأحد الطرفين فقط.
• اليوم 7 — حل أسئلة نصوص الرأي في اختبار.
• اليوم 14 — راجع إشارات الطرفين قبل الاختبار.

🧭 تكنيكة اسما (ASMA) للقراءة:
• (أ) أول سطر: اقرأ أول سطر من كل فقرة لتخمين الموضوع العام.
• (س) السؤال: اقرأ السؤال وحدد الكلمات الدلالية (Keywords).
• (م) مكان الإجابة: ابحث بعينيك عن الكلمات الدلالية واقرأ جملتها بتركيز.
• (ا) استبعد الاختيارات الخاطئة والبعيدة لتأكيد الصحيح.
• ابدأ بأسئلة المعنى والضمائر أولاً، وابحث عن المرادفات (Synonyms).
• ممنوع: قراءة القطعة بأسلوب الجرائد، أو الحفظ/المعلومات العامة، أو ترك أي سؤال فارغ.', array['q-reading-13','q-reading-14']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-listening-3', 'course-listening', 'محادثات في الفنادق والجامعات', 4, '[]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
في محادثات الخدمات، الحل النهائي الذي يقترحه المتحدث الثاني بعد الاعتذار أو التوضيح هو محور السؤال.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• اسمع الاعتذار/التوضيح أولًا (Sorry, Let me check).
• بعدها يظهر الحل المقترح — هو الإجابة الصحيحة غالبًا.

⚠️ فخ STEP:
الخيار الأول المطروح قد يكون غير متاح (مغلق، محجوز)؛ السؤال يسأل عن الحل الفعلي النهائي.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — حدد الحل النهائي في حوار خدمة.
• اليوم 3 — أجب عن سؤال "ماذا قرر المتحدث؟".
• اليوم 7 — حل أسئلة الفنادق والمواعيد في اختبار.
• اليوم 14 — راجع عبارات الحلول (I can offer, How about).', array['q-listening-13','q-listening-16']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-writingAnalysis-3', 'course-writingAnalysis', 'أخطاء الفاصلة والملكية والنفي المزدوج', 2, '[]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
وصل جملتين مستقلتين بفاصلة فقط دون حرف عطف يُسمى comma splice وهو خطأ؛ والملكية للمفرد بإضافة ''s وليس s''؛ والنفي المزدوج خطأ دائمًا.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• فاصلة بين جملتين مستقلتين = خطأ (أضف and/because أو فصلهما).
• ملكية مفرد: the student''s book (لا the students'' إلا للجمع).
• don''t have no → don''t have any.

⚠️ فخ STEP:
الخيارات الخاطئة تعرض النفي المزدوج أو ملكية خاطئة بصيغة تبدو صحيحة؛ افحص كل عنصر في الجملة.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — حدد comma splice في 3 جمل.
• اليوم 3 — صحّح نفيًا مزدوجًا.
• اليوم 7 — حل أسئلة الفاصلة والملكية في اختبار.
• اليوم 14 — راجع الحالات الثلاث بمثال لكل منها.', array['q-writing-9','q-writing-10']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-vocabulary-3', 'course-vocabulary', 'مفردات من نصوص القراءة الجديدة', 2, '[]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
كلمات مثل benchmark وarable وentrepreneur تتكرر في نصوص القراءة الحديثة عن التعليم والتقنية والزراعة، وراجعتها في سياقها لا منفردة.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• اربط الكلمة بموضوعها: benchmark ← المعايير، arable ← الزراعة.
• احفظ جملة واحدة لكل كلمة جديدة.

⚠️ فخ STEP:
الكلمات المتشابهة شكلًا (entrepreneur/enterprise) تُخلط إن حُفظت دون سياق؛ ثبّت كل كلمة داخل جملة مميزة.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — احفظ الكلمات الثلاث بجمل.
• اليوم 3 — أعد كتابة الجمل من الذاكرة.
• اليوم 7 — حل سؤال مفردات من نصوص حديثة.
• اليوم 14 — راجع الجمل السياقية قبل الاختبار.', array[]::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-tips-3', 'course-tips', 'يوم الاختبار: نصائح أخيرة', 2, '[]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
قراءة التعليمات كاملة قبل كل قسم تمنع خسارة أسئلة بسب تعديل بسيط، والتنفس الهادئ أسرع طريقة لمعالجة التوتر.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• اقرأ تعليمات كل قسم حتى لو بدت مألوفة.
• عند التوتر: توقف وتنفس 5 ثوانٍ ثم واصل.

⚠️ فخ STEP:
القلق يبطئ سرعة القراءة ويسبب أخطاء اندفاعية؛ جملة "درست هذه الأنماط من قبل" تعيد تركيزك.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — مرر على تعليمات الأقسام.
• اليوم 3 — درّب التنفس في اختبار قصير.
• اليوم 7 — طبّق الروتين في اختبار كامل.
• اليوم 14 — راجع قائمة يوم الاختبار.', array[]::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-strategies-3', 'course-strategies', 'التعامل مع القلق أثناء الاختبار', 2, '[]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
القلق في منتصف الاختبار يُعالج بوقف قصير لا بمتابعة القراءة دون استيعاب.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• توقف 5 ثوانٍ وخذ نفسًا عميقًا عند التوتر.
• ذكّر نفسك أنك دربت الأنماط نفسها من قبل.

⚠️ فخ STEP:
متابعة القراءة وأنت قلق تجعل الأخطاء تتراكم؛ الوقوف القصير يعيد التركيز أسرع من الاستمرار.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — تعلّم إشارة التوتر لديك.
• اليوم 3 — درّب التنفس العميق في اختبار.
• اليوم 7 — طبّقها في اختبار كامل.
• اليوم 14 — راجع روتين إدارة القلق.', array[]::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-revision-3', 'course-revision', 'مراجعة أسبوعية ٣', 2, '[]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
الجولة الثالثة تركّز على التراكيب والمفردات الأحدث لتثبيتها قبل الانتقال لأسبوع جديد.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• حل أسئلة التراكيب والمفردات الجديدة أولًا.
• راجع فجواتك من الجولات السابقة.

⚠️ فخ STEP:
إهمال الأخطاء الأخيرة يجعلها تعود في الاختبار الرسمي؛ أنهاها الآن.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — حل الجولة الثالثة.
• اليوم 3 — راجع التراكيب الجديدة فقط.
• اليوم 7 — أعد حل الأخطاء الثلاثة الأخيرة.
• اليوم 14 — استعد للأسبوع الجديد بثقة.', array['q-grammar-19','q-reading-16','q-listening-14','q-writing-11']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-grammar-4', 'course-grammar', 'الجمل الشرطية المتقدمة والانقلاب', 11, '[{"type":"youtube","url":"https://www.engvid.com/mixed-verb-tenses-in-english-conditionals-and-if-clauses/","title":"Mixed Verb Tenses in English: Conditionals and IF clauses","provider":"engVid","youtubeId":"hk1AvFgOsSo"},{"type":"youtube","url":"https://www.engvid.com/advanced-english-grammar-word-order-inversions/","title":"Advanced English Grammar: Word Order Inversions","provider":"engVid","youtubeId":"TO2CtYcFYWg"}]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
الجمل الشرطية المتقدمة تحذف If عندما تبدأ بـ Had أو Were أو Should، والأدوات مثل No sooner...than وOnly after تُجبر الجملة على الانقلاب.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• Had I known ← If I had known.
• No sooner + ماضي تام + than (No sooner had she arrived than...).
• Only after + جملة + انقلاب للجملة الرئيسية.

⚠️ فخ STEP:
بعد Had/No sooner/Only after يجب انقلاب الفعل المساعد مع الفاعل. ولا تخلط بين "No sooner...than" و"No sooner...when" — الصحيح than فقط.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — حوّل جملة شرطية تبدأ بـ If إلى Had.
• اليوم 3 — كوّن جملة بـ No sooner...than.
• اليوم 7 — حل أسئلة الانقلاب في اختبار تجريبي.
• اليوم 14 — اكتب فقرة فيها شرط ثالث كامل مع انقلاب.

🧭 تكنيكة اجدا (AGDA) للجرامر:
• (أ) اقرأ الاختيارات أولاً لتحديد القاعدة المستهدفة (لا تبدأ بالسؤال).
• (ج) خمّن القاعدة النحوية: أزمنة؟ مقارنة؟ حروف جر؟ مبني للمجهول؟ شرطية؟...
• (د) حدد الكلمات الدلالية في الجملة (مفاتيح زمنية أو تركيبية).
• (ا) استبعد الخيارات غير المتوافقة نحواً أو زمناً.
• ⏱️ قاعدة الـ45 ثانية: إن لم تصل للإجابة خلال 45 ثانية خمّن وضع إجابة وانتقل فوراً، وحلّ بالترتيب دون قفز.', array['q-grammar-42','q-grammar-38','q-grammar-45']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-reading-4', 'course-reading', 'قراءة نصوص البيئة والتلوث', 6, '[]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
نصوص البيئة تبني علاقة سبب ونتيجة بين الجمل، وأسئلتها تدور غالبًا حول "لماذا" و"ماذا يقترح الباحثون".

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• اربط كل نتيجة بسببها: البلاستيك الدقيق ← ينتقل للحيوانات ← يصل للإنسان.
• جواب سؤال "ماذا يقترحون" عادة جملة واحدة في وسط النص.

⚠️ فخ STEP:
لا تخلط بين السبب والنتيجة: اسأل "ما الذي أدّى إلى هذه النتيجة؟" وليس "ما النتيجة النهائية؟".

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — ارسم سلسلة سبب←نتيجة لنص بيئي.
• اليوم 3 — أجب عن سؤال "لماذا" بالرجوع للسبب.
• اليوم 7 — حل أسئلة التلوث وإعادة التدوير.
• اليوم 14 — راجع مفردات البيئة المرتبطة بالأسباب.

🧭 تكنيكة اسما (ASMA) للقراءة:
• (أ) أول سطر: اقرأ أول سطر من كل فقرة لتخمين الموضوع العام.
• (س) السؤال: اقرأ السؤال وحدد الكلمات الدلالية (Keywords).
• (م) مكان الإجابة: ابحث بعينيك عن الكلمات الدلالية واقرأ جملتها بتركيز.
• (ا) استبعد الاختيارات الخاطئة والبعيدة لتأكيد الصحيح.
• ابدأ بأسئلة المعنى والضمائر أولاً، وابحث عن المرادفات (Synonyms).
• ممنوع: قراءة القطعة بأسلوب الجرائد، أو الحفظ/المعلومات العامة، أو ترك أي سؤال فارغ.', array['q-reading-27','q-reading-28','q-reading-29']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-listening-4', 'course-listening', 'الاشتراكات والعروض الترويجية', 5, '[]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
في محادثات الاشتراكات، محور السؤال: ما الذي تضيفه الباقة الأغلى؟ ولماذا يختار المتحدث في النهاية؟

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• قارن بين ما تشمله الباقة الأساسية وما تضيفه الأغلى.
• السبب النهائي للاختيار يُذكر عادة مرة واحدة (سعر أو ميزة).

⚠️ فخ STEP:
الخيار الخاطئ يصف ميزة الباقة التي لم يخترها المتحدث؛ ثبّت اختياره النهائي قبل الجواب.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — قارن باقة أساسية وأغلى في حوار.
• اليوم 3 — أجب عن سؤال سبب الاختيار النهائي.
• اليوم 7 — حل أسئلة النوادي والخدمات في اختبار.
• اليوم 14 — راجع تعابير المقارنة (includes, plus, for extra).', array['q-listening-27','q-listening-28','q-listening-29']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-writingAnalysis-4', 'course-writingAnalysis', 'أخطاء الوصل: الفاصلة المقطوعة', 3, '[]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
جملتان كاملتان بينهما فاصلة فقط دون حرف عطف = comma splice، والتصحيح إما بفصل الجملتين أو بإضافة أداة ربط.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• ربط: جملة، and جملة (It was late, and we left).
• سببية: جملة because جملة (We left because it was late).
• فصل: نقطة أو فاصلة منقوطة.

⚠️ فخ STEP:
الخيار الصحيح يعالج الرابط، ولا يكتفي بإعادة ترتيب الكلمات مع بقاء الفاصل الفعلي نفسه.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — اكتشف الفاصلة المقطوعة في جملة.
• اليوم 3 — أصلحها بطريقتين مختلفتين.
• اليوم 7 — حل أسئلة الوصل في اختبار تجريبي.
• اليوم 14 — راجع أدوات الربط قبل الاختبار.', array['q-writing-19']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-vocabulary-4', 'course-vocabulary', 'مفردات نصوص البيئة والعلوم', 3, '[]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
كلمات مثل decompose وingest وmicroplastic وcompound تظهر في نصوص البيئة، وارتباطها بسياقها (التحلل، الابتلاع) يثبّتها أفضل من الحفظ المجرد.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• decompose ← البلاستيك يتحلل.
• ingest ← الحيوانات تبتلع الجزيئات.
• compound ← مركب كيميائي.

⚠️ فخ STEP:
كلمة واحدة قد تحمل معنيين (compound: مركب/يضاعف)؛ حدد المعنى من السياق لا من الحفظ.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — اربط 4 كلمات بسياقاتها البيئية.
• اليوم 3 — حل سؤال مفردات من نص بيئة.
• اليوم 7 — استخدم الكلمات في جمل عن التلوث.
• اليوم 14 — راجع قائمة مفردات البيئة.', array[]::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-grammar-5', 'course-grammar', 'التفضيلات المترابطة وso/such', 12, '[{"type":"youtube","url":"https://www.engvid.com/so-such/","title":"Conversational English – So & Such","provider":"engVid","youtubeId":"T86QtX2XZeg"},{"type":"youtube","url":"https://www.engvid.com/english-grammar-modifying-comparatives/","title":"English Grammar: Modifying Comparatives","provider":"engVid","youtubeId":"Fr83ObluJ2A"}]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
تركيب "The + تفضيل، the + تفضيل" يعبّر عن علاقة طردية بين أمرين، و"So + صفة + that" يعبّر عن نتيجة.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• The more you practice, the better you become.
• So + صفة + that (The book was so long that...).
• Such + اسم + that (It was such a long book that...).

⚠️ فخ STEP:
لا تستخدم that مع So مباشرة بلا صفة بينهما، ولا تخلط Such مع So: such تتبعها جملة اسمية، وso تتبعها صفة فقط.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — كوّن جملتين بـ the...the.
• اليوم 3 — حل سؤال so/such في القسم الكتابي.
• اليوم 7 — اكتب جملة نتيجة بـ such...that.
• اليوم 14 — راجع الفرق بين so/such بمثالين في الاختبار.

🧭 تكنيكة اجدا (AGDA) للجرامر:
• (أ) اقرأ الاختيارات أولاً لتحديد القاعدة المستهدفة (لا تبدأ بالسؤال).
• (ج) خمّن القاعدة النحوية: أزمنة؟ مقارنة؟ حروف جر؟ مبني للمجهول؟ شرطية؟...
• (د) حدد الكلمات الدلالية في الجملة (مفاتيح زمنية أو تركيبية).
• (ا) استبعد الخيارات غير المتوافقة نحواً أو زمناً.
• ⏱️ قاعدة الـ45 ثانية: إن لم تصل للإجابة خلال 45 ثانية خمّن وضع إجابة وانتقل فوراً، وحلّ بالترتيب دون قفز.', array['q-grammar-30','q-grammar-34','q-grammar-41']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-reading-5', 'course-reading', 'الاستدلال من النصوص العلمية', 7, '[]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
سؤال الاستدلال (inference) لا تُكتب إجابته في النص، بل تُبنى من مجموع الجمل.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• اجمع 2-3 أدلة من النص تؤدي لاستنتاج واحد.
• اقرأ الجملة الأخيرة من الفقرة بتمعن — الاستنتاجات تُبنى غالبًا على خلاصة الكاتب.

⚠️ فخ STEP:
إن وُجدت الإجابة كجملة حرفية في النص فهي غالبًا إجابة سؤال آخر (صريح)، وليست استنتاجًا صحيحًا.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — استخرج استنتاجًا من دليلين في نص.
• اليوم 3 — حل سؤال inference دون الرجوع للنص الحرفي.
• اليوم 7 — حل أسئلة النصوص العلمية في اختبار.
• اليوم 14 — راجع مؤشرات الاستدلال والنتائج الختامية.

🧭 تكنيكة اسما (ASMA) للقراءة:
• (أ) أول سطر: اقرأ أول سطر من كل فقرة لتخمين الموضوع العام.
• (س) السؤال: اقرأ السؤال وحدد الكلمات الدلالية (Keywords).
• (م) مكان الإجابة: ابحث بعينيك عن الكلمات الدلالية واقرأ جملتها بتركيز.
• (ا) استبعد الاختيارات الخاطئة والبعيدة لتأكيد الصحيح.
• ابدأ بأسئلة المعنى والضمائر أولاً، وابحث عن المرادفات (Synonyms).
• ممنوع: قراءة القطعة بأسلوب الجرائد، أو الحفظ/المعلومات العامة، أو ترك أي سؤال فارغ.', array['q-reading-30','q-reading-34']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-listening-5', 'course-listening', 'عمليات الشراء والإرجاع', 6, '[]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
في محادثات الشراء والإرجاع: نوع الحل المختار (استرداد، استبدال، رصيد) هو محور السؤال، وسماع سؤال البائع يكشف شرط السياسة.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• اسمع نوع الحل النهائي الذي يوافق عليه المتحدث.
• سؤال البائع (Do you have the receipt?) = شرط في سياسة الإرجاع.

⚠️ فخ STEP:
الخيار الخاطئ يذكر استبدالًا بينما المتحدث قبل الاسترداد؛ ركّز على الفعل النهائي المتفق عليه.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — حدد نوع الحل في حوار إرجاع.
• اليوم 3 — أجب عن سؤال شرط السياسة.
• اليوم 7 — حل أسئلة الشراء والإرجاع في اختبار.
• اليوم 14 — راجع مفردات الإرجاع (refund, exchange, receipt).', array['q-listening-30','q-listening-31','q-listening-32']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-writingAnalysis-5', 'course-writingAnalysis', 'الكلمات المتشابهة: affect/effect وamount/number', 4, '[]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
affect فعل بمعنى يؤثر، وeffect اسم بمعنى أثر؛ amount للمواد غير المعدودة وnumber للمعدودة.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• affect = يتسبب في تغيير (The weather affects mood).
• effect = نتيجة/أثر (The effect was noticeable).
• amount of + غير معدود (amount of water).
• number of + معدود (number of students).

⚠️ فخ STEP:
الخيارات تستبدل أحدهما بالآخر عمدًا؛ اسأل: هل المقصود فعل أم اسم؟ وهل الاسم معدود أم لا؟

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — اكتب جملة بـ affect وأخرى بـ effect.
• اليوم 3 — حل سؤال amount مقابل number.
• اليوم 7 — حل أسئلة المتشابهات في اختبار.
• اليوم 14 — راجع الأزواج المتشابهة قبل الاختبار.', array['q-writing-20','q-writing-26']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-vocabulary-5', 'course-vocabulary', 'مفردات محادثات الاستماع اليومية', 4, '[]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
محادثات الاستماع تدور حول الاشتراكات والمواعيد والمطارات؛ حفظ مرادفاتها العملية (membership, exchange, connecting flight) وربطها بالسيناريو يرفع درجتك.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• membership ← النوادي والاشتراكات.
• exchange ← الإرجاع والاستبدال.
• boarding pass ← المطار.

⚠️ فخ STEP:
الخيار الخاطئ يستخدم كلمة من السيناريو نفسه لكن بمعنى آخر؛ اسمع السياق كاملًا لا الكلمة فقط.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — احفظ مرادفات السيناريوهات الثلاثة.
• اليوم 3 — ربط الكلمة بالسيناريو عند سماعها.
• اليوم 7 — حل أسئلة استماع ومفردات معًا.
• اليوم 14 — راجع قائمة مفردات المحادثات.', array[]::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-grammar-6', 'course-grammar', 'المبني للمجهول عبر الأزمنة', 5, '[{"type":"youtube","url":"https://www.engvid.com/the-passive-when-why-and-how-to-use-it/","title":"The Passive: When, why, and how to use it","provider":"engVid","youtubeId":"C6pHfjH0Efg"},{"type":"youtube","url":"https://www.engvid.com/easy-introduction-to-passive/","title":"Easy Introduction to PASSIVE","provider":"engVid","youtubeId":"QTQb42w7oig"}]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
المبني للمجهول يتكون دائمًا من فعل be بالزمن المطلوب + اسم المفعول، ويُستخدم حين لا يُذكر من يقوم بالفعل.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• المستقبل: will be + اسم المفعول (will be published).
• الماضي: was/were + اسم المفعول (was published).
• التام: has/had been + اسم المفعول (has been announced).

⚠️ فخ STEP:
افحص أولًا: هل الفاعل مذكور بعد by؟ إن لم يُذكر فالجملة مجهولة غالبًا. ولا تنسَ أن be نفسها تتغير مع الزمن وليس فقط الفعل الرئيسي.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — حدد زمن الجملة قبل تحويلها للمجهول.
• اليوم 3 — حول 3 جمل بأزمنة مختلفة.
• اليوم 7 — حل أسئلة المجهول في الاختبار التجريبي.
• اليوم 14 — راجع التام المجهول (has been) بمثال من القراءة.

🧭 تكنيكة اجدا (AGDA) للجرامر:
• (أ) اقرأ الاختيارات أولاً لتحديد القاعدة المستهدفة (لا تبدأ بالسؤال).
• (ج) خمّن القاعدة النحوية: أزمنة؟ مقارنة؟ حروف جر؟ مبني للمجهول؟ شرطية؟...
• (د) حدد الكلمات الدلالية في الجملة (مفاتيح زمنية أو تركيبية).
• (ا) استبعد الخيارات غير المتوافقة نحواً أو زمناً.
• ⏱️ قاعدة الـ45 ثانية: إن لم تصل للإجابة خلال 45 ثانية خمّن وضع إجابة وانتقل فوراً، وحلّ بالترتيب دون قفز.', array['q-grammar-11','q-grammar-44','q-grammar-3']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-reading-6', 'course-reading', 'نصوص الصحة والسلوك', 8, '[]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
نصوص الصحة والسلوك تقدّم مفهومًا عامًا ثم تدعمه بنتائج دراسات؛ والسؤال قد يطلب أيًا منهما.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• ميّز المفهوم النظري (فكرة) عن النتيجة العملية (رقم/نتيجة دراسة).
• اسأل: هل يطلب السؤال "ما يقوله النص عمومًا" أم "ما وجدته الدراسة"؟

⚠️ فخ STEP:
الخيارات تخلط بين المفهوم ونتيجة الدراسة؛ عندما تذكر الجملة دراسةً معينة، اربط الخيار بنتيجتها تحديدًا.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — لخّص نصًا صحيًا إلى (فكرة + نتيجة دراسة).
• اليوم 3 — أجب عن سؤال يستهدف النتيجة العملية.
• اليوم 7 — حل أسئلة النوم والعادات في اختبار.
• اليوم 14 — راجع مصطلحات الدراسات (study, findings, researchers).

🧭 تكنيكة اسما (ASMA) للقراءة:
• (أ) أول سطر: اقرأ أول سطر من كل فقرة لتخمين الموضوع العام.
• (س) السؤال: اقرأ السؤال وحدد الكلمات الدلالية (Keywords).
• (م) مكان الإجابة: ابحث بعينيك عن الكلمات الدلالية واقرأ جملتها بتركيز.
• (ا) استبعد الاختيارات الخاطئة والبعيدة لتأكيد الصحيح.
• ابدأ بأسئلة المعنى والضمائر أولاً، وابحث عن المرادفات (Synonyms).
• ممنوع: قراءة القطعة بأسلوب الجرائد، أو الحفظ/المعلومات العامة، أو ترك أي سؤال فارغ.', array['q-reading-31','q-reading-32','q-reading-33']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-listening-6', 'course-listening', 'السفر وتأجيل الرحلات', 7, '[]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
في محادثات السفر ثلاثة محاور للأسئلة: سبب التأخير، الحل المقدم، وطريقة استلام المعلومات الجديدة.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• اسمع سبب التأخير (طقس، عطل، اكتظاظ).
• اسمع الحل ثم وسيلة الإبلاغ (بريد، رسالة، مكتب).

⚠️ فخ STEP:
السؤال قد يستهدف وسيلة الإبلاغ لا السبب؛ حدد أي عنصر من الثلاثة يسأل عنه السؤال.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — التقط عناصر الثلاثة في حوار سفر.
• اليوم 3 — أجب عن سؤال وسيلة الإبلاغ.
• اليوم 7 — حل أسئلة المطارات في اختبار.
• اليوم 14 — راجع مفردات السفر (delay, reroute, notify).', array['q-listening-33','q-listening-34','q-listening-35']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-writingAnalysis-6', 'course-writingAnalysis', 'اتفاق الضمير مع مرجعه', 5, '[]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
كلمات مثل Each وEveryone وAnybody مفردة نحويًا، فيتبعها ضمير مفرد (his or her) وليس جمعًا.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• Every student should bring his or her own copy.
• Anyone who finishes early may leave.

⚠️ فخ STEP:
الضمير في الجملة يرجع إلى الفاعل لا إلى الاسم القريب؛ حدد الفاعل أولًا ثم احكم على الضمير.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — حدد الفاعل قبل الضمير في 3 جمل.
• اليوم 3 — صحّح ضمير جمعًا بعد Everyone.
• اليوم 7 — حل أسئلة الضمائر في اختبار تجريبي.
• اليوم 14 — راجع قائمة الكلمات المفردة دائمًا.', array['q-writing-21','q-writing-25']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-vocabulary-6', 'course-vocabulary', 'كلمات مترادفة ترفع درجتك', 5, '[]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
المرادفات تمنحك مرونة: consolidate بدل strengthen، وingest بدل swallow — كل مرادف يوسّع فهمك للنصوص الجديدة.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• استبدل الكلمة البسيطة بمرادف أكاديمي داخل الجملة.
• احفظ المرادف ضمن الجملة الأصلية لا منفردًا.

⚠️ فخ STEP:
المرادفات ليست متطابقة دائمًا (affect vs effect)؛ تحقق من الجزء من الكلام (اسم/فعل) قبل الاستبدال.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — كوّن 3 جمل بمرادفات أكاديمية.
• اليوم 3 — استبدل كلمة بسيطة بمرادفها في فقرة.
• اليوم 7 — حل سؤال مرادف في سياق قراءة.
• اليوم 14 — راجع قائمة المرادفات قبل الاختبار.', array[]::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-grammar-7', 'course-grammar', 'الجمل الاسمية والفعل بعد الاقتراح', 10, '[{"type":"youtube","url":"https://www.engvid.com/advanced-english-grammar-noun-clauses/","title":"Advanced English Grammar: Noun Clauses","provider":"engVid","youtubeId":"9SrEEPt4MQA"},{"type":"youtube","url":"https://www.engvid.com/easy-english-conversation-suggest-recommend/","title":"Easy English Conversation: SUGGEST & RECOMMEND","provider":"engVid","youtubeId":"OIqDAF8szq4"}]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
بعد أفعال الاقتراح (suggest, recommend) مع that، يأتي الفعل بصيغة المصدر المجرد (base form) دون تصريف.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• suggest/recommend + that + فاعل + فعل مجرد (suggest that the report be submitted).
• بعد حرف الجر: فعل + ing (object to having).

⚠️ فخ STEP:
لا تصرّف الفعل بعد suggest that في الغائب (لا تقل suggests that he submits، بل submit). وأفعال مثل object to تتطلب دائمًا -ing بعد حرف الجر.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — اكتب جملة بـ suggest that + فعل مجرد.
• اليوم 3 — حل سؤال فعل بعد حرف الجر.
• اليوم 7 — كوّن جملة بـ object to having.
• اليوم 14 — راجع قائمة أفعال الاقتراح قبل الاختبار.

🧭 تكنيكة اجدا (AGDA) للجرامر:
• (أ) اقرأ الاختيارات أولاً لتحديد القاعدة المستهدفة (لا تبدأ بالسؤال).
• (ج) خمّن القاعدة النحوية: أزمنة؟ مقارنة؟ حروف جر؟ مبني للمجهول؟ شرطية؟...
• (د) حدد الكلمات الدلالية في الجملة (مفاتيح زمنية أو تركيبية).
• (ا) استبعد الخيارات غير المتوافقة نحواً أو زمناً.
• ⏱️ قاعدة الـ45 ثانية: إن لم تصل للإجابة خلال 45 ثانية خمّن وضع إجابة وانتقل فوراً، وحلّ بالترتيب دون قفز.', array['q-grammar-37','q-grammar-43']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-reading-7', 'course-reading', 'نصوص الخدمات العامة والمجتمع', 9, '[]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
نصوص الخدمات العامة تقارن الماضي بالحاضر أو تعرض تحدٍّ ماليًا، وأسئلتها المتكررة: من المستفيد الأكثر؟ وما التهديد الرئيسي؟

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• ابحث عن الجماعة التي تُخدم (المرتادون، منخفضو الدخل).
• ابحث عن التهديد الرئيسي (ميزانية، إغلاق فروع).

⚠️ فخ STEP:
لا تخلط بين "الخدمة المقدمة" و"التهديد الذي تواجهه" — ركّز على كلمة السؤال (خدمة/تهديد/تحدٍّ).

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — حدد المستفيد والتهديد في نص خدمة.
• اليوم 3 — أجب عن سؤال المستفيد الأكثر.
• اليوم 7 — حل أسئلة المكتبات والنقل في اختبار.
• اليوم 14 — راجع مفردات الخدمات (municipal, branch, funding).

🧭 تكنيكة اسما (ASMA) للقراءة:
• (أ) أول سطر: اقرأ أول سطر من كل فقرة لتخمين الموضوع العام.
• (س) السؤال: اقرأ السؤال وحدد الكلمات الدلالية (Keywords).
• (م) مكان الإجابة: ابحث بعينيك عن الكلمات الدلالية واقرأ جملتها بتركيز.
• (ا) استبعد الاختيارات الخاطئة والبعيدة لتأكيد الصحيح.
• ابدأ بأسئلة المعنى والضمائر أولاً، وابحث عن المرادفات (Synonyms).
• ممنوع: قراءة القطعة بأسلوب الجرائد، أو الحفظ/المعلومات العامة، أو ترك أي سؤال فارغ.', array['q-reading-35','q-reading-36']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-listening-7', 'course-listening', 'المكالمات الرسمية والمقابلات', 8, '[]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
في المكالمات الرسمية، ترتيب الأحداث هو مفتاح الإجابة: الموعد المقترح أولًا ثم ما يفعله المتحدث بعده.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• اسمع الموعد/الوقت المقترح.
• اسمع الفعل التالي (يرسل بريدًا، يؤكد حضورًا) بعد الاتصال.

⚠️ فخ STEP:
السؤال يسأل "ماذا يفعل المتحدث بعد الاتصال؟" — الجواب هو الفعل اللاحق وليس الموعد نفسه.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — رتّب أحداث مكالمة بالترتيب.
• اليوم 3 — أجب عن سؤال الفعل اللاحق.
• اليوم 7 — حل أسئلة المكالمات الرسمية في اختبار.
• اليوم 14 — راجع تعابير المتابعة (then I''ll, after that).', array['q-listening-36','q-listening-37','q-listening-38']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-writingAnalysis-7', 'course-writingAnalysis', 'العبارات المعلّقة والتوازي', 6, '[]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
عبارة افتتاحية (مثل Walking down the street) يجب أن تصف الفاعل الذي يليها مباشرة، وإلا كانت معلقة (dangling).

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• Opening phrase + فاعل + فعل: يجب أن يكون الفاعل هو من يفعل عبارة الافتتاح.
• Walking down the street, I saw... (الصحيح) — لا the building.

⚠️ فخ STEP:
الخيار الخاطئ يضع فاعلًا لا يستطيع القيام بالفعل الافتتاحي؛ اسأل: من الذي فعل هذا الفعل؟

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — حدد الفاعل الصحيح لعبارة معلقة.
• اليوم 3 — صحّح عبارة افتتاحية خاطئة.
• اليوم 7 — حل أسئلة العبارات المعلقة في اختبار.
• اليوم 14 — راجع بنية الافتتاح قبل الاختبار.', array['q-writing-22','q-writing-23']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-vocabulary-7', 'course-vocabulary', 'فهم الكلمة من سياقها', 6, '[]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
لا تبحث عن كل كلمة جديدة في القاموس؛ خمّن معناها من الجمل المحيطة أولًا، فالشرح أو المعاكس يأتي غالبًا بعدها مباشرة.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• ابحث عن مرادف أو تعريف في الجملة التالية.
• ابحث عن معاكس (however, unlike) في الجملة نفسها.

⚠️ فخ STEP:
السؤال قد يسأل عن معنى الكلمة من السياق فقط؛ لو حفظت معنى واحدًا فقط قد يفشل أمام معنى سياقي مختلف.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — خمّن معنى كلمة من سطرين محيطين.
• اليوم 3 — تحقق من تخمينك بالقاموس.
• اليوم 7 — حل أسئلة معنى الكلمة من السياق.
• اليوم 14 — راجع إشارات السياق قبل الاختبار.', array[]::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-grammar-8', 'course-grammar', 'أدوات العطف المترابطة والتوازي', 9, '[{"type":"youtube","url":"https://www.engvid.com/english-grammar-correlative-conjunctions/","title":"English Grammar: Correlative Conjunctions (NEITHER & NOR, EITHER & OR, BOTH & AND…)","provider":"engVid","youtubeId":"XYYUXskbX_c"}]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
أدوات العطف المترابطة تأتي كزوج ثابت لا ينفصل، وحافظ على نفس الصيغة النحوية لكل عناصر القائمة (توازي).

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• not only...but also، neither...nor، either...or.
• بين عناصر القائمة: فعل + فعل أو اسم + اسم بنفس الصيغة.

⚠️ فخ STEP:
لا تكسر الزوج الثابت (لا تقل not only...but...also). وعند الاتفاق مع neither...nor: الفعل يتبع الفاعل الأقرب (Neither the teacher nor the students were...).

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — راجع أزواج العطف الثلاثة.
• اليوم 3 — حل سؤال اتفاق فعل مع neither...nor.
• اليوم 7 — صحّح جملة مكسورة التوازي.
• اليوم 14 — حل أسئلة النحو والكتابة المترابطة معًا.

🧭 تكنيكة اجدا (AGDA) للجرامر:
• (أ) اقرأ الاختيارات أولاً لتحديد القاعدة المستهدفة (لا تبدأ بالسؤال).
• (ج) خمّن القاعدة النحوية: أزمنة؟ مقارنة؟ حروف جر؟ مبني للمجهول؟ شرطية؟...
• (د) حدد الكلمات الدلالية في الجملة (مفاتيح زمنية أو تركيبية).
• (ا) استبعد الخيارات غير المتوافقة نحواً أو زمناً.
• ⏱️ قاعدة الـ45 ثانية: إن لم تصل للإجابة خلال 45 ثانية خمّن وضع إجابة وانتقل فوراً، وحلّ بالترتيب دون قفز.', array['q-grammar-13','q-grammar-39','q-grammar-32']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-reading-8', 'course-reading', 'تحديد رأي الكاتب من النص', 10, '[]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
رأي الكاتب يُستشف من صيغ التقييم والإشارات: حين يمدح طرفًا ويضعف المقابل، فموقفه واضح.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• ابحث عن صيغ التقييم: more valuable، counterproductive، critical، flawed.
• اسأل: "من الذي تخدمه الصفات الإيجابية في النص؟"

⚠️ فخ STEP:
النص قد يذكر حجج الطرفين لكن موقف الكاتب يظهر في اختيار كلماته لا في ذكره للحجج.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — علّم صيغ التقييم في نص رأي.
• اليوم 3 — حدد موقف الكاتب من صيغة تقييم واحدة.
• اليوم 7 — حل أسئلة رأي الكاتب في اختبار.
• اليوم 14 — راجع قائمة صيغ التقييم قبل الاختبار.

🧭 تكنيكة اسما (ASMA) للقراءة:
• (أ) أول سطر: اقرأ أول سطر من كل فقرة لتخمين الموضوع العام.
• (س) السؤال: اقرأ السؤال وحدد الكلمات الدلالية (Keywords).
• (م) مكان الإجابة: ابحث بعينيك عن الكلمات الدلالية واقرأ جملتها بتركيز.
• (ا) استبعد الاختيارات الخاطئة والبعيدة لتأكيد الصحيح.
• ابدأ بأسئلة المعنى والضمائر أولاً، وابحث عن المرادفات (Synonyms).
• ممنوع: قراءة القطعة بأسلوب الجرائد، أو الحفظ/المعلومات العامة، أو ترك أي سؤال فارغ.', array['q-reading-37','q-reading-38']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-listening-8', 'course-listening', 'استخراج التفاصيل الدقيقة', 9, '[]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
بعض الأسئلة تسأل عن تفصيل ذُكر مرة واحدة فقط (رقم، شرط، موقع)، ويظهر عادة في النصف الثاني من رد الموظف.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• ركّز على النصف الثاني من إجابة الموظف/البائع.
• التفصيل المطلوب (شرط مثل إبراز الإيصال) يأتي متأخرًا في الجملة.

⚠️ فخ STEP:
لا تجب من أول الحوار؛ التفاصيل المطلوبة تظهر آخرًا حيث يستكمل المتحدث معلوماته.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — علّم الشرط/الرقم في رد موظف.
• اليوم 3 — أجب عن سؤال شرط واحد مذكور مرة.
• اليوم 7 — حل أسئلة التفاصيل الدقيقة في اختبار.
• اليوم 14 — راجع أسئلة "what do you need to bring".', array['q-listening-39','q-listening-40','q-listening-41']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-writingAnalysis-8', 'course-writingAnalysis', 'الجماعات والأسماء الجمعية في اتفاق الفعل', 7, '[]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
الاسم الجمعي (team, committee) مفرد في المعنى المتصل؛ وas well as أو along with لا تحوّل الفاعل إلى جمع؛ وفعل الوصل who يتبع الاسم السابق له مباشرة.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• The team is playing well (اسم جمعي ← فعل مفرد).
• The teacher, as well as the students, is ready.
• one of the students who have... (who يرجع للجمع).

⚠️ فخ STEP:
العبارة الاعتراضية بين فاصلتين لا تغير عدد الفاعل؛ حدد الفاعل الأساسي قبل الفاصلة.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — حدد فاعل جملة فيها as well as.
• اليوم 3 — صحّح فعل جمع بعد اسم جمعي.
• اليوم 7 — حل أسئلة الجماعات في اختبار.
• اليوم 14 — راجع قاعدة who مع السابق المباشر.', array['q-writing-24','q-writing-28','q-writing-29']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-vocabulary-8', 'course-vocabulary', 'المراجعة النهائية للمفردات', 7, '[]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
راجع بطاقاتك الأقدم أولًا قبل الجديدة، فالمفردات المنسية تحتاج إعادة تثبيت أكثر من الحديثة.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• ابدأ من البطاقات المنسية (الأقدم) ثم الجديدة.
• خصص 10 دقائق يوميًا لمراجعة التكرار المتباعد.

⚠️ فخ STEP:
مراجعة الكلمات الحديثة وحدها تُعطي إحساسًا خاطئًا بالتقدم؛ الكلمات القديمة المنسية هي التي ترفع مستواك.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — راجع البطاقات المنسية أولًا.
• اليوم 3 — أعد تثبيت 5 كلمات نسيان.
• اليوم 7 — حل اختبار مفردات شامل.
• اليوم 14 — مراجعة نهائية قبل الاختبار التجريبي.', array[]::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-grammar-9', 'course-grammar', 'الجمل الشرطية: الأنواع الثلاثة', 3, '[{"type":"youtube","url":"https://www.engvid.com/learn-english-grammar-the-4-conditionals/","title":"Learn English Grammar: The 4 Conditionals","provider":"engVid","youtubeId":"veTo6eWJQrM"},{"type":"youtube","url":"https://www.engvid.com/learn-english-grammar-3rd-conditional/","title":"Learn English Grammar: The 3rd Conditional","provider":"engVid","youtubeId":"-VkYM6SyJSE"}]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
الجملة الشرطية تعبّر عن علاقة شرط/نتيجة، ولكل نوع زمن خاص به: الأول للحقائق والمستقبل المحتمل، والثاني للافتراضات غير الواقعية، والثالث للتأسف على الماضي.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• النوع الأول: If + مضارع بسيط، will + فعل مجرد.
• النوع الثاني: If + ماضٍ بسيط، would + فعل مجرد.
• النوع الثالث: If + ماضٍ تام، would have + اسم المفعول.

⚠️ فخ STEP:
لا تضع would في جملة if نفسها (لا تقل If I would study). وكلمة wish عن الماضي تتبع قاعدة النوع الثالث: I wish I had studied.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — صنّف 3 جمل شرطية حسب نوعها.
• اليوم 3 — أكمل جملة من النوع الثاني.
• اليوم 7 — حوّل wish ماضوي إلى would have.
• اليوم 14 — حل أسئلة الشرط في اختبار تجريبي كامل.

🧭 تكنيكة اجدا (AGDA) للجرامر:
• (أ) اقرأ الاختيارات أولاً لتحديد القاعدة المستهدفة (لا تبدأ بالسؤال).
• (ج) خمّن القاعدة النحوية: أزمنة؟ مقارنة؟ حروف جر؟ مبني للمجهول؟ شرطية؟...
• (د) حدد الكلمات الدلالية في الجملة (مفاتيح زمنية أو تركيبية).
• (ا) استبعد الخيارات غير المتوافقة نحواً أو زمناً.
• ⏱️ قاعدة الـ45 ثانية: إن لم تصل للإجابة خلال 45 ثانية خمّن وضع إجابة وانتقل فوراً، وحلّ بالترتيب دون قفز.', array['q-grammar-4','q-grammar-35','q-grammar-42']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-grammar-10', 'course-grammar', 'المقارنة والتفضيل', 1, '[{"type":"youtube","url":"https://www.engvid.com/english-grammar-comparative-adjectives/","title":"English Grammar – Comparative Adjectives","provider":"engVid","youtubeId":"HqueToxC4d4"},{"type":"youtube","url":"https://www.engvid.com/english-grammar-superlative-adjectives/","title":"Learn English Grammar: Superlative Adjectives","provider":"engVid","youtubeId":"uMhE2dp1rko"}]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
للمقارنة بين شيئين نستخدم صيغة المقارنة (comparative)، وللتمييز بين أكثر من شيئين نستخدم التفضيل (superlative) مع أداة التعريف the.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• المساواة: as + صفة + as (النفي: not as...as).
• المقارنة: صفة أقصر + er / more + صفة أطول.
• التفضيل: the + الأقصر + est / the most + الأطول.
• good ← better ← the best.

⚠️ فخ STEP:
لا تقل more good بل better. ولا تخلط between as...as و so...that: as تستخدم للمساواة فقط، وso تحتاج أن تتبعها نتيجة بـ that.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — قارن شيئين بـ as...as.
• اليوم 3 — حل سؤال تفضيل مع the.
• اليوم 7 — صحّح more good إلى better في جملة.
• اليوم 14 — راجع صيغ المقارنة غير المنتظمة قبل الاختبار.

🧭 تكنيكة اجدا (AGDA) للجرامر:
• (أ) اقرأ الاختيارات أولاً لتحديد القاعدة المستهدفة (لا تبدأ بالسؤال).
• (ج) خمّن القاعدة النحوية: أزمنة؟ مقارنة؟ حروف جر؟ مبني للمجهول؟ شرطية؟...
• (د) حدد الكلمات الدلالية في الجملة (مفاتيح زمنية أو تركيبية).
• (ا) استبعد الخيارات غير المتوافقة نحواً أو زمناً.
• ⏱️ قاعدة الـ45 ثانية: إن لم تصل للإجابة خلال 45 ثانية خمّن وضع إجابة وانتقل فوراً، وحلّ بالترتيب دون قفز.', array['q-grammar-6','q-grammar-15','q-grammar-23']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-grammar-11', 'course-grammar', 'حروف الجر وdespite/in spite of', 2, '[{"type":"youtube","url":"https://www.engvid.com/how-prepositions-actually-work-english-grammar/","title":"How Prepositions Actually Work | English Grammar","provider":"engVid","youtubeId":"ee9MGLIBl-A"},{"type":"youtube","url":"https://www.engvid.com/how-to-express-opposing-ideas-in-english/","title":"How to express opposing ideas in English: despite, although, nevertheless, in spite of…","provider":"engVid","youtubeId":"UFuxlnUeGmg"}]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
حروف الجر تتبع الكلمة التي تسبقها، وأدوات التخالف (despite / in spite of) تأخذ اسمًا بعدها، بينما although / even though تأخذ جملة كاملة.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• responsible for / good at / object to (فعل + جر محدد).
• Despite + اسم (Despite the rain...).
• In spite of + اسم (In spite of his experience...).
• Although + جملة (Although it rained...).

⚠️ فخ STEP:
لا تتبع despite بجملة كاملة، ولا تنسَ of مع in spite of. وبعد حروف الجر دائمًا: فعل + ing (good at solving).

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — راجع الأزواج: responsible for, good at.
• اليوم 3 — حل سؤال despite مقابل although.
• اليوم 7 — اكتب 3 جمل بـ in spite of.
• اليوم 14 — راجع قائمة حروف الجر الشائعة في الاختبار.

🧭 تكنيكة اجدا (AGDA) للجرامر:
• (أ) اقرأ الاختيارات أولاً لتحديد القاعدة المستهدفة (لا تبدأ بالسؤال).
• (ج) خمّن القاعدة النحوية: أزمنة؟ مقارنة؟ حروف جر؟ مبني للمجهول؟ شرطية؟...
• (د) حدد الكلمات الدلالية في الجملة (مفاتيح زمنية أو تركيبية).
• (ا) استبعد الخيارات غير المتوافقة نحواً أو زمناً.
• ⏱️ قاعدة الـ45 ثانية: إن لم تصل للإجابة خلال 45 ثانية خمّن وضع إجابة وانتقل فوراً، وحلّ بالترتيب دون قفز.', array['q-grammar-16','q-grammar-24','q-grammar-26','q-grammar-36','q-grammar-40']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-grammar-12', 'course-grammar', 'المصدر الصريح والصفة الفعلية', 4, '[{"type":"youtube","url":"https://www.engvid.com/gerund-or-infinitive/","title":"English Grammar – Gerund or Infinitive?","provider":"engVid","youtubeId":"zPzjJHKyNwc"},{"type":"youtube","url":"https://www.engvid.com/advanced-english-grammar-participles/","title":"Advanced English Grammar: Participles","provider":"engVid","youtubeId":"eWvkSp16VFY"}]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
بعض الأفعال تأخذ المصدر الصريح (to do) وبعضها يلزم الصفة الفعلية (doing) وبعضها يقبل الاثنين، والفروق محفوظة وتُسأل كثيرًا في STEP.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• أفعال تأخذ -ing فقط: enjoy, suggest, avoid, mind, finish.
• بعد حرف الجر دائمًا: -ing (object to having).
• suggest + that + فعل مجرد: suggest that the report be.

⚠️ فخ STEP:
لا تقل suggest to do بل suggest doing. ولا تستخدم to بعد حرف الجر أبدًا — حرف الجر يتبعه فعل -ing فقط.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — احفظ قائمة أفعال -ing.
• اليوم 3 — حل سؤال enjoy + -ing.
• اليوم 7 — كوّن جملة بـ object to having.
• اليوم 14 — راجع الفرق بين to + مصدر و -ing بمثالين.

🧭 تكنيكة اجدا (AGDA) للجرامر:
• (أ) اقرأ الاختيارات أولاً لتحديد القاعدة المستهدفة (لا تبدأ بالسؤال).
• (ج) خمّن القاعدة النحوية: أزمنة؟ مقارنة؟ حروف جر؟ مبني للمجهول؟ شرطية؟...
• (د) حدد الكلمات الدلالية في الجملة (مفاتيح زمنية أو تركيبية).
• (ا) استبعد الخيارات غير المتوافقة نحواً أو زمناً.
• ⏱️ قاعدة الـ45 ثانية: إن لم تصل للإجابة خلال 45 ثانية خمّن وضع إجابة وانتقل فوراً، وحلّ بالترتيب دون قفز.', array['q-grammar-9','q-grammar-29','q-grammar-43']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-grammar-13', 'course-grammar', 'الأزمنة المتقدمة والكلام المنقول', 7, '[{"type":"youtube","url":"https://www.engvid.com/master-english-time-perfect-tenses/","title":"Master English Time: HAVE, HAVE HAD, HAD HAD, WILL HAVE HAD","provider":"engVid","youtubeId":"IA2jZAA7JjM"},{"type":"youtube","url":"https://www.engvid.com/learn-english-grammar-indirect-speech-reported-speech/","title":"Learn English Grammar: INDIRECT SPEECH (REPORTED SPEECH)","provider":"engVid","youtubeId":"cgHZvDzi78U"}]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
المستقبل التام (future perfect) يصف حدثًا يكتمل قبل نقطة مستقبلية، والكلام المنقول يزيح الأزمنة خطوة للخلف عندما يكون فعل النقل ماضيًا.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• المستقبل التام: will have + اسم المفعول (will have departed).
• By the time + مضارع، جملة رئيسية مستقبل تام.
• كلام منقول ماضي: have seen ← had seen، will ← would.

⚠️ فخ STEP:
مع By the time الزمن الصحيح هو will have + ماضٍ تام وليس المضارع التام. وفي الكلام المنقول، لا تنسَ إزاحة الزمن خطوة للخلف مع فعل نقل ماضي.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — كوّن جملة بـ will have completed.
• اليوم 3 — انقل سؤالًا من المباشر إلى المنقول.
• اليوم 7 — حل سؤال By the time في الاختبار.
• اليوم 14 — راجع جدول إزاحة الأزمنة قبل الاختبار.

🧭 تكنيكة اجدا (AGDA) للجرامر:
• (أ) اقرأ الاختيارات أولاً لتحديد القاعدة المستهدفة (لا تبدأ بالسؤال).
• (ج) خمّن القاعدة النحوية: أزمنة؟ مقارنة؟ حروف جر؟ مبني للمجهول؟ شرطية؟...
• (د) حدد الكلمات الدلالية في الجملة (مفاتيح زمنية أو تركيبية).
• (ا) استبعد الخيارات غير المتوافقة نحواً أو زمناً.
• ⏱️ قاعدة الـ45 ثانية: إن لم تصل للإجابة خلال 45 ثانية خمّن وضع إجابة وانتقل فوراً، وحلّ بالترتيب دون قفز.', array['q-grammar-27','q-grammar-31','q-grammar-33']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-grammar-14', 'course-grammar', 'الجمل الوصلية وسؤال الذيل', 6, '[{"type":"youtube","url":"https://www.engvid.com/learn-english-grammar-the-adjective-clause-relative-clause/","title":"Learn English Grammar: The Adjective Clause (Relative Clause)","provider":"engVid","youtubeId":"GpV39YEmh5k"},{"type":"youtube","url":"https://www.engvid.com/english-grammar-tag-questions/","title":"English Grammar – Tag Questions","provider":"engVid","youtubeId":"jY46m3rhxwk"}]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
الجمل الوصلية (relative clauses) تربط جملة باسم سابق، وسؤال الذيل (question tag) يعكس الفعل المساعد وفاعل الجملة الأصلية.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• whose للملكية: the engineer whose design won.
• who للأشخاص، which للأشياء، where للأماكن.
• سؤال الذيل: جملة مثبتة + tag منفي (have finished → haven''t you).

⚠️ فخ STEP:
لا تستخدم which للأشخاص ولا whose بمعنى who. وسؤال الذيل يتبع الفعل المساعد في الجملة، وليس الزمن المعنى: You''ve finished → haven''t you وليس didn''t you.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — ميّز whose عن who/which.
• اليوم 3 — كوّن سؤال ذيل لجملة مثبتة.
• اليوم 7 — حل أسئلة الوصل والذيل معًا.
• اليوم 14 — راجع ضمائر الوصل الخمسة قبل الاختبار.

🧭 تكنيكة اجدا (AGDA) للجرامر:
• (أ) اقرأ الاختيارات أولاً لتحديد القاعدة المستهدفة (لا تبدأ بالسؤال).
• (ج) خمّن القاعدة النحوية: أزمنة؟ مقارنة؟ حروف جر؟ مبني للمجهول؟ شرطية؟...
• (د) حدد الكلمات الدلالية في الجملة (مفاتيح زمنية أو تركيبية).
• (ا) استبعد الخيارات غير المتوافقة نحواً أو زمناً.
• ⏱️ قاعدة الـ45 ثانية: إن لم تصل للإجابة خلال 45 ثانية خمّن وضع إجابة وانتقل فوراً، وحلّ بالترتيب دون قفز.', array['q-grammar-5','q-grammar-19','q-grammar-28','gx-grammar-7','gx-grammar-8']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-reading-9', 'course-reading', 'أنواع أسئلة القراءة الخمسة وترتيب الحل', 0, '[]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
مهما اختلفت القطعة فأسئلة القراءة خمسة أنواع فقط: المعنى، الضمير، المعلومة، الموضوع، الرأي. لا تحلها بترتيب ظهورها؛ ابدأ بالمعنى والضمير لأن مكان كل منهما محدد في النص (كلمة بخط عريض أو تحتها خط)، ثم المعلومة، ثم الموضوع، ثم الرأي.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• المعنى: تراه مع mean أو closest in meaning ← اقرأ الجملة المحيطة بالكلمة.
• الضمير: يسأل what does it/they refer to ← اقرأ ما قبل الضمير فقط.
• المعلومة: لها كلمات دلالية في السؤال ← ابحث عنها في النص وأجب من جملتها.
• الموضوع: main idea / best title / purpose ← أول وآخر جملة من الفقرة، أو أول جملة من كل فقرة.
• الرأي: author''s attitude / view ← ابحث عن كلمات الحكم مثل unfortunately وshould وremarkable.

⚠️ فخ STEP:
خيار يذكر معلومة صحيحة وردت في النص لكنها جزء من الفكرة لا الفكرة العامة. في أسئلة الموضوع اختر الخيار الأشمل.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — صنّف عشرة أسئلة إلى أنواعها الخمسة دون حلها.
• اليوم 3 — حل أسئلة المعنى والضمير أولًا في قطعة كاملة.
• اليوم 7 — طبّق الترتيب الكامل في اختبار بوقت محدد.
• اليوم 14 — راجع الأنواع التي أخطأت فيها أكثر.

🧭 بالتكامل مع تكنيكة اسما (ASMA):
• (أ) أول سطر من كل فقرة، ثم (س) السؤال وكلماته الدلالية، ثم (م) مكان الإجابة، ثم (ا) استبعد الخيارات.
• الوقت التقريبي: دقيقة ونصف لكل سؤال شاملةً قراءة القطعة، فلا تقرأها كالجريدة.
• لا تترك سؤالًا فارغًا؛ إذا انتهى وقتك فخمّن.', array['q-reading-43','sm-reading-15','q-reading-45','q-reading-48','q-reading-22']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-reading-10', 'course-reading', 'أسئلة المعنى والضمير: الإجابة من السياق', 1, '[]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
سؤال المعنى والضمير لا يحتاج قراءة القطعة كلها؛ اذهب إلى الكلمة المحددة، واقرأ جملتها، وخمّن المعنى أو المرجع بنفسك قبل أن تنظر إلى الخيارات.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• المعنى: اقرأ الجملة قبل الكلمة وبعدها، وابحث عن إشارة (سبب، تضاد، مثال) تكشف معناها، ثم اختر أقرب خيار لتخمينك.
• الضمير (he, she, it, they, this, which): يعود إلى اسم مذكور قبله؛ اقرأ من بداية الجملة إلى الضمير وتجاهل ما بعده.
• طابق العدد: they تعود إلى جمع، وit إلى مفرد غير عاقل.

⚠️ فخ STEP:
خيار يشبه الكلمة في الشكل أو الصوت لا في المعنى، وخيار ضمير يعود إلى اسم أبعد من المرجع الصحيح. كلمة risk تعني مخاطرة وقد تظهر بجانب danger؛ المعنى يُحدَّد بالسياق لا بالترجمة الأولى.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — خمّن معاني خمس كلمات من سياقها قبل النظر إلى الخيارات.
• اليوم 3 — حل ثلاثة أسئلة ضمائر وتحقق من العدد.
• اليوم 7 — حلها ضمن قطعة بوقت محدد.
• اليوم 14 — راجع أي سؤال أخطأت فيه من هذا النوع.

🧭 تطبيق اسما: في هذين النوعين تُحدَّد الكلمة في السؤال، فالخطوة الثانية والثالثة شبه جاهزة؛ ركّز على الخطوة الرابعة (استبعاد الخيارات).', array['q-reading-43','q-reading-46','q-reading-49','q-reading-52','sm-reading-15','sm-reading-31']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-reading-11', 'course-reading', 'أسئلة الموضوع والرأي: الفكرة العامة لا الجزئية', 2, '[]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
سؤال الموضوع يطلب الفكرة التي تغطي الفقرة أو القطعة كلها، وسؤال الرأي يطلب موقف الكاتب المفهوم من اختيار ألفاظه. الخيار الصحيح غالبًا هو الأعم، لا التفصيل الذي يذكره النص بحروفه.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• موضوع فقرة: اقرأ أول جملة وآخر جملة وخمّن الفكرة الكبرى.
• موضوع القطعة: اقرأ أول جملة في كل فقرة وربطها معًا.
• الرأي: ابحث عن كلمات الحكم والتقييم (should, unfortunately, remarkable, concern) وعن صيغ التفضيل.
• استبعد الخيار الذي يغطي فقرة واحدة فقط.

⚠️ فخ STEP:
خيار صحيح معلوماتيًا لكنه جزئي. اسأل نفسك: هل يغطي القطعة كلها أم جزءًا منها؟ وفي الرأي لا تُسقط رأيك الشخصي أو معلوماتك العامة؛ اعتمد على النص.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — لخّص فقرة في عبارة قصيرة ثم قارنها بالخيارات.
• اليوم 3 — حل سؤالَي موضوع ورأي.
• اليوم 7 — طبّق الأسلوب على قطعة كاملة بوقت محدد.
• اليوم 14 — راجع الأسئلة التي اخترت فيها خيارًا جزئيًا.

🧭 تطبيق اسما: الخطوة الأولى (أول سطر من كل فقرة) هي الأهم هنا لأنها تبني الصورة العامة.', array['q-reading-26','q-reading-34','q-reading-45','q-reading-48','q-reading-63','q-reading-78']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-listening-9', 'course-listening', 'روتين الـ15 ثانية قبل المقطع', 0, '[]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
قبل كل مقطع يُعطيك الاختبار وقتًا قصيرًا (نحو 15 ثانية لكل سؤال) لقراءة الأسئلة. هذه ثواني ذهبية: استعملها لتعرف بالضبط ما تبحث عنه، حتى تكون أذنك جاهزة لالتقاط الإجابة وقت سماعها.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• اقرأ السؤال وخمّن موضوعه (من؟ متى؟ أين؟ لماذا؟).
• حدّد الخلاصة: كلمة أو كلمتان تدلانك على الإجابة.
• مرّ على الخيارات بكلمة دلالية في كل خيار فقط، ولا تقرأها كاملة.
• اترك عينيك على الأسئلة وأنت تستمع.

⚠️ فخ STEP:
قراءة الخيارات كاملة تضيّع وقتك، وتُشتّتك عن بداية المقطع. الأسئلة تأتي بترتيب الكلام نفسه.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — درّب نفسك على استخراج الخلاصة من خمسة أسئلة دون سماع.
• اليوم 3 — طبّق الروتين على مقطع واحد وأجب.
• اليوم 7 — جرّب الروتين مع قسم استماع كامل.
• اليوم 14 — راجع الأسئلة التي ضاعت منك إجابتها.

🧭 تذكّر: لا تدوّن ملاحظات. ركّز على السماع وعلى الأسئلة أمامك.', array['q-listening-1','q-listening-10','q-listening-11','q-listening-13']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-listening-10', 'course-listening', 'إعادة الصياغة واستبعاد الخيارات أثناء السماع', 1, '[]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
المتحدث نادرًا يقول الإجابة بنفس ألفاظ الخيار؛ يُعيد صياغتها بكلمات أخرى. تعلّم أن تلتقط المعنى لا اللفظ، واستبعد الخيارات أولًا بأول أثناء الكلام.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• إذا سمعت كلمة دلالية من السؤال الثاني فقد انتهى السؤال الأول، فانتقل.
• قارن ما تسمع بالخيارات بعينيك واشطب ما قيل عكسه.
• المعلومة الصحيحة غالبًا تأتي بعد كلمات التصحيح مثل actually وbut.
• في الاستنتاج (inference) اجمع ما قيل ولا تعتمد على جملة واحدة.

⚠️ فخ STEP:
الخيار الذي يحتوي نفس الكلمات المسموعة قد يكون الفخ، والصحيح هو ما يطابق المعنى بكلمات مختلفة. وإذا فاتك سؤال فلا تتحسر عليه؛ تابع حتى لا تفقد الذي بعده.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — اقرأ ثلاثة أسئلة، وفكّر في كيف يمكن إعادة صياغة إجاباتها.
• اليوم 3 — حل مقطعًا واستبعد الخيارات أثناء السماع.
• اليوم 7 — حل سؤال استنتاج بالتركيز على المعنى.
• اليوم 14 — راجع مقاطع أخطأت فيها بسبب فخ التكرار اللفظي.', array['q-listening-3','q-listening-12','q-listening-32']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-grammar-15', 'course-grammar', 'المعدود وغير المعدود وكلمات الكمية', 14, '[{"type":"youtube","url":"https://www.engvid.com/countable-uncountable-nouns/","title":"English Grammar Tricks – Countable & Uncountable Nouns","provider":"engVid","youtubeId":"YsagocS1wGo"},{"type":"youtube","url":"https://www.engvid.com/learn-english-grammar-few-little-a-few-a-little/","title":"Learn English Grammar: FEW, LITTLE, A FEW, A LITTLE","provider":"engVid","youtubeId":"kjzr-hTNqdQ"}]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
الاسم المعدود يأخذ many وfew وعددًا (a student, two students)، وغير المعدود يأخذ much وlittle ولا يُجمع ولا يسبقه a. أسماء مثل information وadvice وnews وfurniture وequipment وluggage وhomework غير معدودة.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• How many + معدود جمع؛ How much + غير معدود.
• a few / few + معدود؛ a little / little + غير معدود.
• some في الإثبات، any في النفي والسؤال، ولا نقول informations أو an advice.
• a lot of تصلح للاثنين.

⚠️ فخ STEP:
few وlittle بدون a تعني "قليل يكاد لا يكفي" (معنى سلبي)، أما a few وa little فتعني "قليل لكنه كافٍ". تحقق من المعنى المطلوب.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — صنّف عشر كلمات إلى معدود وغير معدود.
• اليوم 3 — حل سؤالَي How much / How many.
• اليوم 7 — حل أسئلة الكمية في اختبار القواعد.
• اليوم 14 — راجع الاستثناءات التي أخطأت فيها.

🧭 تكنيكة اجدا (AGDA): انظر إلى الخيارات أولًا؛ إذا رأيت many وmuch وfew وlittle فاعلم أن السؤال عن المعدود وغير المعدود، ثم انظر إلى الاسم بعد الفراغ.', array['sm-grammar-4','sm-grammar-5']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-grammar-16', 'course-grammar', 'wish: تمني الحاضر والماضي', 15, '[{"type":"youtube","url":"https://www.engvid.com/speaking-english-i-wish-i-had/","title":"Speaking English – “I wish I had…”","provider":"engVid","youtubeId":"T4c0J8BiVvg"},{"type":"youtube","url":"https://www.engvid.com/wish-subjunctive/","title":"English Grammar – “I wish…” – Subjunctive","provider":"engVid","youtubeId":"-vefS0B1DIY"}]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
بعد wish نرجع بالزمن خطوة إلى الوراء: تمنٍّ عن الحاضر يأخذ الماضي البسيط، وندم على الماضي يأخذ الماضي التام، وانزعاج من أمر مستمر يأخذ would + الفعل.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• حاضر مخالف للواقع: I wish I had a car (ليس عندي سيارة). مع be نستخدم were لكل الضمائر: I wish I were taller.
• ندم على الماضي: I wish I had studied harder (لم أذاكر جيدًا).
• ضيق من موقف أو رغبة في تغييره: I wish he would stop talking.

⚠️ فخ STEP:
لا نستخدم المضارع ولا will بعد wish (خطأ: I wish I have / I wish I will). وانتبه لكلمات الزمن: yesterday وlast month وبالأمس تعني الماضي التام، وnow تعني الماضي البسيط.

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — حوّل خمس جمل واقعية إلى تمنٍّ بـ wish.
• اليوم 3 — ميّز بين wish + past وwish + past perfect في أسئلة.
• اليوم 7 — حل أسئلة wish ضمن اختبار القواعد.
• اليوم 14 — راجع الفرق بين wish وif (الشرطية الثانية والثالثة).

🧭 تكنيكة اجدا (AGDA): إذا رأيت wish في الجملة فانظر في الخيارات إلى الأزمنة؛ المضارع غير مقبول بعد wish، ثم حدّد هل المطلوب حاضر أم ماضٍ من الكلمات الدلالية.', array['gx-grammar-1','gx-grammar-2','gx-grammar-3']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.lessons (id, course_id, title, "order", resources, notes, question_ids) values ('lesson-grammar-17', 'course-grammar', 'used to وbe used to وget used to', 16, '[{"type":"youtube","url":"https://www.engvid.com/i-used-to-i-got-used-to-im-used-to-whats-the-difference/","title":"“I USED TO”, “I GOT USED TO”, “I’M USED TO”: What’s the difference?!","provider":"engVid","youtubeId":"DmLVH5Y096Q"},{"type":"youtube","url":"https://www.engvid.com/used-to-be-used-to-get-used-to/","title":"USED TO, BE USED TO, GET USED TO: When and How to Use Them","provider":"engVid","youtubeId":"ymq-OAaSK08"}]'::jsonb, '📌 القاعدة المركزية:
ثلاث تراكيب متشابهة الشكل مختلفة المعنى: used to + الفعل المجرد لعادة ماضية انتهت، وbe used to + اسم أو -ing بمعنى معتاد على، وget used to + اسم أو -ing بمعنى يتعوّد على.

⚡ الصيغة السريعة:
• I used to play football (كنت ألعب، والآن لا).
• I am used to getting up early (اعتدت ذلك).
• I am getting used to the new city (أتعوّد تدريجيًا).
• النفي والسؤال: did not use to / Did you use to...؟ (من غير d).

⚠️ فخ STEP:
بعد be used to وget used to لا يأتي الفعل المجرد؛ نقول used to living وليس used to live. وكلمة now في الجملة تدل غالبًا على عادة ماضية انتهت (used to).

🔄 مراجعة متباعدة:
• اليوم 1 — ضع خطًا تحت التراكيب الثلاثة في فقرة قصيرة.
• اليوم 3 — اختر بين used to وbe used to في خمس جمل.
• اليوم 7 — حل أسئلة used to ضمن اختبار.
• اليوم 14 — راجع فخ الفعل المجرد بعد be used to.

🧭 تكنيكة اجدا (AGDA): انظر في الخيارات إلى الفروق بين used to وis used to وget used to؛ ثم ابحث في الجملة عن الفعل بعد الفراغ: إن كان -ing فالتركيب be/get used to، وإن كان مجردًا فالتركيب used to.', array['gx-grammar-4','gx-grammar-5','gx-grammar-6']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set course_id = excluded.course_id, title = excluded.title, "order" = excluded.order, resources = excluded.resources, notes = excluded.notes, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-water-scarcity', 'Water Scarcity in Arid Regions', 'Many countries in arid and semi-arid regions face a persistent challenge: the demand for fresh water consistently outpaces the natural supply. Traditional solutions, such as building dams and drilling deeper wells, have offered only temporary relief, as groundwater reserves take centuries to replenish naturally. In response, several governments have turned to desalination, a process that removes salt from seawater to produce potable water. While desalination plants can supply large coastal cities reliably, the process consumes enormous amounts of energy, and the leftover brine, if not managed carefully, can damage marine ecosystems. As a result, researchers are now focusing on making desalination more energy-efficient and on developing complementary strategies, such as wastewater recycling and public campaigns that encourage households to reduce daily consumption.', array['q-reading-1','q-reading-2','q-reading-3']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-remote-work', 'The Shift Toward Remote Work', 'Before 2020, remote work was often viewed as a rare privilege offered mainly to senior employees. That perception changed rapidly once large numbers of companies were forced to let employees work from home. Many organizations discovered that productivity did not decline as much as they had feared; in some cases, it even improved. However, the shift also revealed new challenges. Employees reported feeling isolated without the casual interactions of a physical office, and some managers struggled to evaluate performance without direct observation. In response, a growing number of companies have adopted hybrid models, requiring employees to come into the office only a few days a week, in an attempt to balance flexibility with the benefits of in-person collaboration.', array['q-reading-4','q-reading-5','q-reading-6']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-electric-vehicles', 'The Rise of Electric Vehicles', 'For decades, the internal combustion engine dominated the automotive industry, but the past ten years have seen a rapid shift toward electric vehicles (EVs). Government incentives, stricter emissions regulations, and falling battery costs have all contributed to this trend. Major automakers have committed billions of dollars to electrifying their vehicle lineups, and several countries have announced plans to ban the sale of new gasoline-powered cars within the next two decades. Despite this momentum, EV adoption still faces obstacles. Charging infrastructure remains limited in many regions, particularly outside major cities, and the batteries themselves require rare minerals whose extraction raises environmental and ethical concerns. Manufacturers are now investing heavily in battery recycling and in developing chemistries that rely less on scarce materials, hoping to make electric vehicles not only cleaner to drive but cleaner to produce.', array['q-reading-7','q-reading-8','q-reading-9']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-multilingual-education', 'The Value of Multilingual Education', 'Educators have long debated the best age to introduce a second language in schools. Some argue that children should focus exclusively on their native language during early education, fearing that early exposure to another language might slow down literacy development. Recent research, however, suggests the opposite: children who learn a second language early often show stronger problem-solving skills and greater cognitive flexibility than their monolingual peers. Multilingual education also appears to have long-term economic benefits, as fluency in more than one language is increasingly valued in a globalized job market. Nevertheless, implementing effective multilingual programs is not simple. It requires well-trained teachers, carefully designed curricula, and sustained investment — resources that many public school systems struggle to provide consistently.', array['q-reading-10','q-reading-11','q-reading-12']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-standardized-testing', 'The Debate Over Standardized Testing', 'For over a century, standardized tests have been used to measure student achievement and to make decisions about university admissions. Supporters argue that these tests provide an objective, consistent measure that allows fair comparisons across large numbers of students, regardless of the school they attended. Critics, however, contend that standardized tests often reward test-taking strategy and short-term memorization more than genuine understanding, and that they can disadvantage students from under-resourced schools who have had less exposure to test-specific coaching. In recent years, some universities have moved to make standardized test scores optional, allowing applicants to be evaluated primarily on grades, essays, and extracurricular achievements instead. Early data on this shift has been mixed: while some institutions report more diverse applicant pools, others note that without a common benchmark, comparing students from vastly different educational backgrounds has become more difficult for admissions officers.', array['q-reading-13','q-reading-14','q-reading-15']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-vertical-farming', 'Vertical Farming in Urban Areas', 'As cities continue to expand and arable land near urban centers becomes scarcer, some entrepreneurs have turned to vertical farming — growing crops in stacked layers inside climate-controlled buildings, often located within or near the cities they supply. Because these farms use LED lighting and precisely controlled irrigation instead of relying on sunlight and rainfall, they can produce crops year-round regardless of outside weather conditions, and they typically use a fraction of the water required by traditional agriculture. Proponents also highlight the reduced transportation distance between farm and consumer, which can lower both costs and carbon emissions associated with shipping produce long distances. Nevertheless, vertical farms face a significant hurdle: the substantial electricity required to power lighting and climate control systems, which can make the produce more expensive than conventionally grown alternatives unless renewable energy sources are used. As the technology matures and energy costs fall, however, many industry analysts expect vertical farming to become an increasingly practical part of urban food supply.', array['q-reading-16','q-reading-17','q-reading-18']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-smart-cities', 'Smart Cities and Urban Data', 'Across the globe, city governments are installing networks of sensors that monitor traffic, waste collection, air quality, and energy use in real time. Proponents argue that these data allow cities to operate far more efficiently: traffic lights can adapt to congestion, garbage trucks can be dispatched only when bins are full, and buildings can automatically reduce heating when rooms are empty. Yet the shift toward data-driven urban management is not without controversy. Critics point out that collecting detailed information about how residents move through a city raises serious privacy concerns, and they warn that the same systems used to improve services could be misused to track individuals without consent. Supporters respond that the data are anonymized and protected by strict regulations, but skeptics maintain that no amount of anonymization fully removes the risk. As a result, many cities now publish the code and the data behind their smart-city programs, hoping that transparency will build the public trust that such systems ultimately depend on.', array['q-reading-19','q-reading-20','q-reading-21','q-reading-22']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sleep-academic-performance', 'Sleep and Academic Performance', 'Students often treat sleep as the first thing they are willing to sacrifice when deadlines approach, yet a growing body of research suggests that this trade-off is counterproductive. During deep sleep, the brain consolidates the information learned during the day, transferring it from short-term to long-term memory. Studies of university students have found that those who regularly sleep fewer than seven hours a night perform noticeably worse on recall tasks than their well-rested peers, even when their study time is identical. Many students try to compensate with caffeine, and while a cup of coffee can temporarily sharpen alertness, researchers note that it cannot replace the memory consolidation that only sleep provides — and consuming it late in the day can actually worsen sleep the following night. Educators increasingly encourage schools to schedule major exams later in the morning, arguing that better-rested students benefit both their grades and their health.', array['q-reading-23','q-reading-24','q-reading-25','q-reading-26']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-ocean-plastic', 'The Crisis of Ocean Plastic', 'Every year, an estimated eight million tons of plastic waste end up in the world''s oceans, where it can persist for centuries. Sunlight and wave action gradually break larger items into tiny fragments known as microplastics, which do not decompose in any practical timeframe. These fragments are frequently mistaken for food by fish, seabirds, and marine mammals, and once ingested they can travel up the food chain, eventually appearing in the seafood people consume. Scientists increasingly find microplastics in drinking water and even in human blood, raising concerns that are not yet fully understood. Governments have responded with bans on single-use plastic bags and straws, and companies have experimented with biodegradable alternatives. Yet researchers warn that recycling alone cannot solve the problem, since plastic production continues to grow faster than waste-management systems can handle it. Many argue that meaningful progress requires reducing the production of plastic in the first place, alongside better recycling infrastructure and international agreements to limit how much waste reaches the sea.', array['q-reading-27','q-reading-28','q-reading-29','q-reading-30']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-habits', 'The Power of Habits', 'Habits are behaviors that have become automatic through repetition, allowing the brain to perform them with little conscious effort. Psychologists describe a simple loop that underlies most habits: a cue, a routine, and a reward. A cue is the trigger that starts the behavior, such as the sight of a phone on the desk; the routine is the action itself, such as picking up the phone; and the reward is the pleasant feeling that follows, which encourages the brain to repeat the loop. Because this cycle is stored deep in the brain, habits are remarkably resistant to change — knowing that a habit is bad is rarely enough to break it. Research suggests that replacing an unwanted routine with a healthier one, while keeping the same cue and reward, is far more effective than trying to eliminate the cue entirely. Forming a new habit also depends heavily on repetition and on the environment: people who design their surroundings to make the desired behavior easy, such as keeping running shoes by the door, are considerably more likely to follow through than those who rely on willpower alone. Over time, small consistent changes compound into large differences in health, productivity, and learning.', array['q-reading-31','q-reading-32','q-reading-33','q-reading-34']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-public-libraries', 'Public Libraries in the Digital Age', 'A century ago, the public library was the primary place where ordinary people could access books without paying for them. Today, with information available instantly on smartphones, some observers have questioned whether libraries still matter. Library professionals respond that their institutions have evolved far beyond lending books. Modern libraries function as community hubs offering free internet access, computer training, job-search assistance, and quiet study space — services that matter most to residents who cannot afford reliable internet at home. They also host children''s programs, language classes, and meeting rooms for local groups, strengthening the social fabric of neighborhoods. Digital resources, such as e-books and online databases, have expanded the library''s reach rather than replaced it. The main threat libraries face is financial: as municipal budgets tighten, branches are sometimes closed or their hours reduced, which disproportionately affects the low-income communities that depend on them most. Supporters argue that in an age of information overload, the library''s role as a trusted, free provider of reliable information is more valuable than ever.', array['q-reading-35','q-reading-36','q-reading-37','q-reading-38']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-urban-green-spaces', 'Urban Green Spaces and Public Health', 'City planners have long viewed parks and gardens mainly as decorative additions to the urban landscape, but a growing body of research argues that they should be treated as essential infrastructure. Studies have found that neighborhoods with greater access to green spaces report lower rates of stress, anxiety, and cardiovascular disease, even after controlling for income and other socioeconomic factors. One explanation is that time spent in nature lowers levels of cortisol, the body''s primary stress hormone, and encourages physical activity such as walking and cycling. Green spaces also cool the surrounding air, which matters as heat waves become more frequent, and they absorb stormwater that would otherwise overwhelm drainage systems. Despite these benefits, low-income neighborhoods tend to have the least access to parks, often because land there is cheaper to develop for housing or industry. Some cities have responded by converting vacant lots into pocket parks and by planting trees along wide boulevards, measures that are relatively inexpensive compared with building new facilities. Researchers caution, however, that simply counting the number of parks is not enough: the quality and safety of a space determine whether residents actually use it, and a neglected park can quickly become a source of disorder rather than health.', array['q-reading-39','q-reading-40','q-reading-41','q-reading-42']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-dna-genetics', 'DNA and the Science of Heredity', 'In 1953, James Watson and Francis Crick proposed the double-helix structure of DNA, the molecule that carries genetic information in living cells. DNA is organized into genes, which act as instructions for building proteins and determining traits such as eye color and height. During cell division, DNA is copied so that each new cell receives a complete set of instructions, and the discovery of how this copying works helped scientists understand how characteristics are passed from parents to offspring. Although every human cell contains roughly the same DNA, variations in specific genes explain why individuals differ, and mutations, or changes in the genetic code, can sometimes lead to diseases such as cancer. Today, researchers use this knowledge in fields ranging from forensic science, where DNA evidence helps identify suspects, to agriculture, where breeders select crops with desirable traits. The ability to read and even edit DNA has transformed medicine, but it also raises ethical questions about how far genetic intervention should go.', array['q-reading-43','q-reading-44','q-reading-45']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-space-junk', 'The Threat of Space Junk', 'Since the launch of the first satellite in 1957, thousands of objects have been placed in orbit around Earth, and many of them are no longer operational. These defunct satellites, spent rocket stages, and fragments from collisions form a growing field of space junk orbiting the planet at enormous speeds. At such velocities, even a small piece of debris can severely damage an active satellite on impact, and researchers warn that a chain reaction of collisions, known as the Kessler syndrome, could eventually make some orbits unusable. Space agencies currently track the largest pieces of debris, but smaller fragments are too numerous to monitor. Several cleanup concepts have been proposed, including nets that capture junk, robotic arms that grab defunct satellites, and materials designed to burn up completely when they re-enter the atmosphere. However, these solutions are expensive, and no international agreement yet requires countries to remove their own debris. As commercial launches multiply, scientists argue that the problem will only worsen unless future spacecraft are designed to be disposed of responsibly.', array['q-reading-46','q-reading-47','q-reading-48']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-earthquake-safety', 'Staying Safe During Earthquakes', 'Earthquakes occur when stress built up along fault lines is suddenly released, sending waves of energy through the ground. Although they cannot be predicted with certainty, scientists can estimate the probability of earthquakes in a region based on its geological history. Safety experts recommend the drop, cover, and hold on technique: people indoors should drop to their hands and knees, cover their head and neck under a sturdy table, and hold on until the shaking stops. Contrary to popular belief, standing in a doorway is no longer recommended, since doorways are often no stronger than the rest of a building. If outdoors, people should move to an open area away from buildings, trees, and power lines. After the shaking stops, the risk of aftershocks and falling debris remains, so experts advise checking for injuries, avoiding damaged structures, and listening to official announcements rather than rumors. Because earthquakes strike without warning, households in active zones are encouraged to prepare emergency kits containing water, food, medication, and important documents well in advance.', array['q-reading-49','q-reading-50','q-reading-51']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-bees-ecosystem', 'Bees and Their Role in the Ecosystem', 'Bees are among the most important pollinators on the planet, and roughly a third of the food humans consume depends on pollination by insects, birds, and bats. As bees move from flower to flower collecting nectar, pollen sticks to their bodies and is transferred between plants, enabling fruits, vegetables, and nuts to develop. In recent decades, however, bee populations in many regions have declined sharply, a phenomenon scientists refer to as colony collapse disorder. Researchers point to several contributing factors, including pesticides, loss of flowering habitats, climate change, and parasites such as the varroa mite. The decline is worrying not only for food production but also for wild plants, many of which rely on bees for reproduction. Conservation efforts now include planting native flowers, reducing the use of harmful chemicals, and supporting beekeepers who maintain healthy colonies. Scientists emphasize that protecting bees benefits agriculture directly, and they note that even small gardens can provide valuable feeding grounds for these insects throughout the growing season.', array['q-reading-52','q-reading-53','q-reading-54']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-paper-making', 'The History of Paper-Making', 'Paper is so common today that its invention is rarely considered, yet the material has a long and remarkable history. The technique of making paper is generally credited to the Chinese court official Cai Lun, who around 105 CE produced sheets from plant fibers soaked in water and pressed flat. The craft gradually spread along trade routes to the Middle East, where papermakers refined the process, and then to Europe, where early paper mills appeared in the twelfth century. For centuries, paper was made by hand from materials such as cotton and linen rags, which limited supply, but the industrial revolution brought machines that could produce long continuous sheets, lowering costs and making paper widely affordable. Modern paper is mostly made from wood pulp, a change that increased production enormously but also raised environmental concerns about deforestation. Today, recycling programs and sustainable forestry practices aim to reduce the industry''s impact, and digital technologies have reduced paper consumption for documents, even as paper remains essential for packaging, books, and hygiene products.', array['q-reading-55','q-reading-56','q-reading-57']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-king-fahd-causeway', 'The King Fahd Causeway', 'The King Fahd Causeway is a remarkable engineering project that connects the eastern coast of Saudi Arabia with the island nation of Bahrain. Construction began in 1982, and the causeway, which consists of five bridges and several artificial islands, was officially opened to traffic in 1986. Stretching about twenty-five kilometers across the Gulf, the structure was designed to withstand harsh marine conditions, including strong currents and shifting sands, and it transformed travel between the two countries by replacing a long ferry journey with a road trip of just over half an hour. The causeway has had a major economic impact, allowing goods and workers to move easily between the two countries and attracting tourism to Bahrain, which many visitors from Saudi Arabia reach by car. The artificial islands built into the structure also serve as customs and border control facilities. Over the years, the causeway has required periodic maintenance to keep its bridges and foundations in good condition, and plans have occasionally been discussed to build a parallel crossing as traffic volumes continue to grow.', array['q-reading-58','q-reading-59','q-reading-60']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-ibn-sina', 'Ibn Sina: A Pioneer of Medicine', 'Ibn Sina, known in the West as Avicenna, was a Persian physician and philosopher whose writings influenced learning for centuries. Born around 980 CE in what is now Uzbekistan, he began studying at an early age and is said to have memorized the Qur''an and mastered several fields while still young. His most famous medical work, the Canon of Medicine, organized medical knowledge of his time into a systematic form and was used as a standard textbook in European universities for over five hundred years. In it, Ibn Sina described infectious diseases, discussed the importance of observing patients, and emphasized the role of hygiene in preventing illness. Beyond medicine, he wrote on logic, mathematics, astronomy, and the nature of the soul, and his philosophy combined the ideas of earlier Greek thinkers with Islamic thought. Although some of his theories were later overturned by modern science, his insistence on careful observation and clear classification shaped the way medicine was practiced and taught for generations. His works were eventually translated into Latin and spread widely across Europe and Asia.', array['q-reading-61','q-reading-62','q-reading-63']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-ice-cream-history', 'The Long History of Ice Cream', 'Ice cream seems like a thoroughly modern treat, but its ancestors date back thousands of years. Ancient Chinese rulers enjoyed flavored ices made from snow and fruit juices, and similar chilled desserts appeared in the Middle East, where syrups flavored with fruit and rose water were cooled with ice. The treat reached Italy through trade and travel, and by the seventeenth century, recipes for frozen cream desserts were circulating among European nobility, who served them at elaborate banquets. Ice cream remained a luxury for the wealthy until the nineteenth century, when improvements in refrigeration and the invention of mechanical freezing machines allowed manufacturers to produce it in large quantities. Around the same time, vendors began selling ice cream on the streets of cities such as New York and London, making it accessible to ordinary people for the first time. Later innovations, including the waffle cone and mass-produced packaged ice cream, helped turn it into a global industry. Today, the dessert is enjoyed in nearly every country, with countless flavors, though historians note that the basic combination of milk, sugar, and ice remains remarkably unchanged from the earliest recipes.', array['q-reading-64','q-reading-65','q-reading-66']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-internet-saudi-arabia', 'The Arrival of the Internet in Saudi Arabia', 'The internet reached Saudi Arabia relatively late compared with many other countries, in large part because the government took time to plan for the service''s introduction. Academic institutions were connected in the mid-1990s through the King Abdulaziz City for Science and Technology, which established an early research network. The public internet was officially launched in 1999, and in the years that followed, the number of users grew rapidly as access prices fell and competition among providers increased. The expansion of broadband connections and, later, mobile networks brought internet access to a large share of the population, and usage became widespread among young people in particular. The growth of the internet has had a broad social impact, changing how Saudis communicate, study, shop, and access government services, many of which moved online. The authorities have also worked to expand internet coverage to remote regions, and Saudi Arabia now ranks among the countries with the highest rates of internet usage, even though penetration figures vary by age and region.', array['q-reading-67','q-reading-68','q-reading-69']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-wwii-impact', 'The Lasting Impact of World War II', 'World War II, fought between 1939 and 1945, was the deadliest conflict in human history, and its consequences shaped the postwar world in lasting ways. The war involved countries on every inhabited continent, and fighting took place on land, at sea, and in the air. Beyond the enormous human cost, the conflict left much of Europe and Asia in ruins, forcing nations to rebuild their economies and cities from the ground up. The war also produced major technological changes: radar, jet engines, and early computers were all developed or refined during the conflict and later found civilian uses. Politically, the war led to the establishment of the United Nations in 1945, an organization created with the aim of preventing future conflicts. It also redrew borders, created new states, and accelerated movements for independence in parts of the colonial world. For the countries that fought on the winning side, the conflict brought both economic growth and new global responsibilities, while defeated nations faced occupation and reconstruction. In this way, the effects of the war continue to be felt in international relations and in the daily lives of millions today.', array['q-reading-70','q-reading-71','q-reading-72']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-istanbul-history', 'The History of Istanbul', 'Few cities have occupied a more important position in world history than Istanbul, which sits on the border between Europe and Asia. The city was founded by Greek settlers around the seventh century BCE and later became Byzantium, an important trading center. Under the Roman emperor Constantine, it was renamed Constantinople and made the capital of the Eastern Roman Empire, where it remained a major center of Christianity for centuries. In 1453, the Ottoman forces under Sultan Mehmed II captured the city, and it became the capital of the Ottoman Empire, a role it held for nearly five centuries. During this period, the city flourished as a center of trade, architecture, and learning, and its skyline gained the domes and minarets for which it is famous today. After the founding of the modern Turkish Republic in 1923, the capital was moved to Ankara, but Istanbul remained the country''s largest and most economically important city. Today, it attracts millions of visitors each year, drawn by monuments that reflect its long history of conquest and renewal.', array['q-reading-73','q-reading-74','q-reading-75']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-indoor-air-pollution', 'Indoor Air Pollution', 'When people think of air pollution, they usually picture factory smokestacks and traffic, yet the air inside homes and offices is often more polluted than outdoor air. Indoor pollutants come from many sources, including cooking fumes, cleaning products, building materials, and smoke from burning fuels. Some of the most common indoor pollutants include carbon monoxide, which can be produced by faulty heaters, and volatile organic compounds, which are released by paints, carpets, and furniture. Because modern buildings are well sealed to save energy, polluted air tends to remain trapped inside, and levels of certain pollutants can become several times higher than those found outdoors. Exposure to these pollutants has been linked to headaches, respiratory problems, and worsening of conditions such as asthma, and researchers have found that the time people spend indoors means the risk is often greater than people realize. Experts recommend practical measures such as opening windows regularly, using exhaust fans in kitchens and bathrooms, choosing low-emission materials, and servicing heating equipment annually to keep pollutant levels low.', array['q-reading-76','q-reading-77','q-reading-78']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-muhammad-ali', 'Muhammad Ali: Champion Inside and Outside the Ring', 'Muhammad Ali, born Cassius Clay in 1942 in Louisville, Kentucky, is widely regarded as one of the greatest boxers of all time. He won a gold medal at the 1960 Olympic Games, and in 1964 he defeated Sonny Liston to become world heavyweight champion, an achievement he repeated several times over a career that lasted more than twenty years. Ali was known not only for his speed and technique in the ring but also for his confident personality, his poetry, and his willingness to speak about social and political issues. His refusal to serve in the American military during the Vietnam War, on religious and moral grounds, led to his boxing license being suspended and cost him several years at the peak of his career, a decision that sparked widespread debate. After he retired, Ali devoted himself to humanitarian causes and traveled widely as a goodwill ambassador, despite developing Parkinson''s disease in his later years. When he died in 2016, he was remembered around the world not simply as an athlete but as a figure who stood up for his beliefs.', array['q-reading-79','q-reading-80','q-reading-81']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-currency-history', 'The History of Coins and Currency', 'Before the invention of money, people exchanged goods directly through barter, a system that was inconvenient because it required each person to want exactly what the other offered. The first known coins appeared in the kingdom of Lydia, in what is now Turkey, around the seventh century BCE, and were made from a naturally occurring mixture of gold and silver called electrum. Because coins were stamped with the authority of the issuing ruler, their weight and value could be trusted, and the practice spread quickly across the Mediterranean and Asia. For most of history, money was based on precious metals, and the value of a coin was closely tied to the metal it contained. Paper money began as a convenient substitute, originally serving as receipts for gold deposited with bankers and later as government-issued notes. In the modern era, most money exists as electronic records in bank accounts, and central banks manage the money supply to keep prices stable. Most recently, digital currencies have appeared, but they remain a small share of the world''s money compared with traditional bank-based systems.', array['q-reading-82','q-reading-83','q-reading-84']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sugar-health-risks', 'The Health Risks of Sugar', 'Sugar is present in a surprising number of everyday foods, from soft drinks and biscuits to sauces and breakfast cereals, and health experts have become increasingly concerned about the amount people consume. The body breaks sugar down into glucose, which it uses for energy, but excess glucose that is not used is stored as fat, and over time a high-sugar diet can lead to weight gain and obesity. Sugar is also closely linked to tooth decay, since bacteria in the mouth feed on sugar and produce acids that damage enamel. More seriously, research has associated high sugar intake with type 2 diabetes and heart disease, and many scientists argue that sugary drinks are a particular concern because they are consumed quickly without making people feel full. Public health agencies now recommend that people limit added sugars to a small share of their total daily calories and suggest replacing sugary drinks with water or unsweetened alternatives. Although sugar is not harmful in very small amounts, experts emphasize that the quantities found in many processed foods make moderation difficult without conscious effort.', array['q-reading-85','q-reading-86','q-reading-87']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm-1', 'DNA and the Birth of Genetics', 'The story of how scientists came to understand DNA begins in 1869, when a Swiss researcher named Friedrich Miescher, working in the German city of Tübingen, pulled an unusual substance out of the nuclei of white blood cells. He gave it the name nuclein, and later investigations showed that this material forms the core of DNA, the molecule built from a sugar-phosphate backbone and four bases that store genetic information. In 1953, James Watson and Francis Crick announced the double-helix structure of DNA, explaining how genetic codes are able to copy themselves. Once this structure was understood, biologists confirmed that nuclein appears in every species, which accounts for the molecule''s stability and its power to pass traits from one generation to the next. The practical benefits soon followed: doctors used knowledge of genes to treat inherited illnesses and to design drugs suited to individual patients, while plant breeders used the same insights to develop crops that resist disease. The discovery therefore links medicine and farming through a single molecule that continues to shape both fields today.', array['sm-reading-1','sm-reading-2','sm-reading-3','sm-reading-4']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm-2', 'The Growing Problem of Space Junk', 'The space around Earth is becoming increasingly crowded with objects that no longer work. Old satellites, empty rocket stages, and pieces left behind by collisions all drift in orbit, and together they form what scientists call space junk. Even very small fragments are dangerous: a single bolt moving at high speed could tear through a solar panel, and engineers regularly change the course of working satellites to avoid a crash. Radar systems currently keep watch on more than forty thousand pieces of debris, though many smaller items remain untracked. To slow the growth of the problem, manufacturers now equip new spacecraft with a small extra engine. When a satellite reaches the end of its life, this engine fires downward so that the craft slows, drops, and burns up completely in the upper atmosphere instead of circling the planet for decades. The Italian Space Agency has warned that solutions must come quickly, arguing that delay could turn low-Earth orbit into a dangerous shooting gallery where one collision triggers others. International cooperation, it says, has to speed up before that nightmare becomes reality.', array['sm-reading-5','sm-reading-6','sm-reading-7','sm-reading-8']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm-3', 'Staying Safe in an Earthquake', 'An earthquake happens when the earth''s crust suddenly shifts, releasing a huge amount of stored energy. The shaking can make buildings collapse, roads crack, and power lines fall, and people who live in areas where earthquakes are common often feel afraid because the events are so hard to predict. Experts say that staying calm and acting quickly is essential when the ground begins to move. If you are inside a building, the best advice is to find the safest route out and leave immediately if you can. As you move, keep away from walls, windows, and heavy objects that could fall on you. Once you are outside, the safest place is an open space far from buildings and other structures that might collapse. It is natural to want to help others, but experts stress that looking after your own safety first is the key to surviving these dangerous moments.', array['sm-reading-9','sm-reading-10','sm-reading-11','sm-reading-12']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm-4', 'Bees and the Ecosystem', 'Bees are insects that are best known for two things: helping flowers produce fruit through pollination and making honey. Thousands of species of bees exist, and some of them live together in large social groups called colonies. These insects can be found on every continent, and their sizes vary greatly, from very tiny kinds to the much larger leaf-cutter bees. One surprising fact is that some bees, sometimes called sniffer bees, can be trained to detect particular substances, which makes them useful to researchers. Bees play a central role in the ecosystem. They make food that other creatures need, and they help trees, flowers, and other plants grow; these plants, in turn, provide food and shelter for many animals. Scientists have also studied the feelings of bees. In one experiment, bees that received an unexpected reward reacted more hopefully to new challenges, while bees that received nothing seemed less enthusiastic.', array['sm-reading-13','sm-reading-14','sm-reading-15','sm-reading-16']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm-5', 'The History of Paper-Making', 'Paper is so common today that it is easy to forget how long it has taken to develop. The material we use now is made by machines: trees are cut down, processed into a mixture called wood pulp, and then pressed and rolled into thin sheets. But the first man-made writing material was not paper. Around four thousand years ago, the ancient Egyptians took the fibrous stems of the papyrus plant, flattened them, laid them crosswise, and pressed them together so that they stuck. Once dried, the result was a piece of papyrus that could be written on. True paper appeared much later, in China around the year 105 CE. A man named Ts''ai Lun found a way to make it from the tough inner bark of the mulberry tree by beating the bark in water to separate the fibres. The mixture was then poured into a bamboo tray and left to dry. Chinese traders eventually carried the knowledge as far as Samarkand, where Arab merchants learned the secret and later brought it to Spain. From there, the craft spread across the rest of Europe.', array['sm-reading-17','sm-reading-18','sm-reading-19','sm-reading-20']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm-6', 'The Climate Zones of Saudi Arabia', 'Saudi Arabia is the largest country in the Middle East, and it covers most of the Arabian Peninsula. Because the kingdom contains three different climatic zones, the weather varies a great deal from one region to another. In the southern highlands, in a city like Abha, temperatures rarely rise above thirty-five degrees Celsius, and Abha receives more rain than any other city in the country, which is why it is known as the wettest place in the kingdom. In contrast, the eastern and central parts of Saudi Arabia suffer the highest number of dust storms each year. In places like Qassim, a dust storm can completely hide the sun, making it almost impossible to see more than a few feet ahead. The western region is different again. Makkah is considered the hottest city in Saudi Arabia, with temperatures that sometimes pass forty-eight degrees in summer. Madinah also has a hot desert climate, with very hot, dry summers and only small amounts of rain, which falls mainly between November and May.', array['sm-reading-21','sm-reading-22','sm-reading-23','sm-reading-24']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm-7', 'The King Fahd Causeway', 'The idea of building a bridge across the Gulf to link Bahrain and Saudi Arabia was first suggested by King Saud in 1954, though it took more than a decade to become a real project. In 1965, Sheikh Khalifa bin Salman Al Khalifa and King Faisal agreed to build the causeway, and in 1968 the two countries formed a joint committee to organize the necessary funding. The bridge was later named after King Fahd, and the name was given by Essa bin Salman Al Khalifa. During the planning stage, the World Bank provided information about the environment and geography of the region, which helped engineers design the structure. The Bahraini side of the bridge is about 4,296 metres long, and the whole crossing carries millions of passengers every year; in 2010 alone, around 19 million people travelled across it. Today the causeway stands as an important symbol of cooperation, and it is often described as a bridge between two nations.', array['sm-reading-25','sm-reading-26','sm-reading-27','sm-reading-28']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm-8', 'The World of Ants', 'There are about eight thousand species of ants in the world, and nearly all of them live in organized groups called colonies. Some ants are actually useful to people because they feed on the eggs and larvae of household insects such as flies, which helps keep homes clean. Ants do not need to see each other to communicate. They use chemical signals called pheromones, along with sounds and touch, and they find food mainly through their sense of smell. They also recognize other ants by smelling them. Ants eat a wide variety of things, including insects, plants, and fruits, and they come in colors such as yellow, brown, red, and black. Red ants, for example, usually live only about twenty-five to thirty days. Some ants are active mainly at night and spend the day resting in houses, palm trees, caves, or wells. Others, called meat ants, have the special job of protecting the nest. In every colony, only the queen can lay eggs, while the worker ants build the nest and collect food.', array['sm-reading-29','sm-reading-30','sm-reading-31','sm-reading-32']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm-9', 'How Animals Sleep', 'Animals sleep in many different ways, and each method helps them survive. Some animals sleep in groups to keep warm; lions, monkeys, and penguins all do this. The way an animal sleeps often depends on its body. Snakes, for example, cannot close their eyes or even blink, so they do not really sleep in the same way other animals do. Horses and cows also sleep with their eyes open, because they have no other choice. Other animals rest standing up. Large elephants sleep standing, while young elephants sleep in the middle of the group, lying down and protected by the bigger animals around them. Horses also sleep standing, and flamingos are famous for sleeping on just one leg. The time of day also matters. Most animals sleep at night, but nocturnal animals such as bats are active during the dark hours and sleep during the day instead.', array['sm-reading-33','sm-reading-34','sm-reading-35','sm-reading-36']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm-10', 'Ibn Sina: A Master of Medicine', 'Ibn Sina, known in the West as Avicenna, is one of the most famous scholars in the history of medicine. The difference between the two names simply reflects the change from one language to another. He was born in the year 980 near Bukhara in Central Asia, where his father managed a village on a royal estate. Ibn Sina began studying medicine at thirteen and became a skilled physician at sixteen, working long hours with his patients. His reputation grew so much that the Sultan of Bukhara, Nuh ibn Mansur, asked him for treatment. When the Sultan recovered, he rewarded Ibn Sina by allowing him to use the royal library, where he could study rare manuscripts. Ibn Sina wrote many books on several subjects. His most important work was an encyclopedia of medicine in five volumes called the Qanun fit Tibb, finished in 1025. He also wrote five books on law and composed poems. His books were translated into many languages, and he continued his work until his death at the age of fifty-six.', array['sm-reading-37','sm-reading-38','sm-reading-39','sm-reading-40']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm2-1', 'How Coral Reefs Form', 'Coral reefs look like underwater gardens, but they are built by animals. Each coral polyp is a tiny, soft creature that produces a hard skeleton of limestone around its body. When polyps die, their skeletons remain, and new polyps grow on top of them. Over thousands of years, these layers pile up into enormous structures. The Great Barrier Reef in Australia, for example, stretches for more than two thousand kilometres and can be seen from space. Reefs grow best in warm, clear, shallow water, because the algae living inside the polyps need sunlight to make food. In return, the algae give the corals their bright colours. Although reefs cover less than one percent of the ocean floor, they shelter about a quarter of all marine species. Sadly, rising sea temperatures cause corals to push out their algae, turning the reef white. This process, called bleaching, can kill an entire reef if the water stays warm for too long. Scientists therefore argue that protecting reefs must begin with limiting the heat that humans add to the planet.', array['sm2-reading-1','sm2-reading-2','sm2-reading-3','sm2-reading-4']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm2-2', 'The Silk Road', 'The Silk Road was not a single road but a network of trade routes linking China with the Mediterranean. It began to flourish around 130 BCE, when Chinese emperors sought strong horses from Central Asia. Merchants rarely travelled the entire distance. Instead, goods passed from trader to trader, and each one added a profit before selling them onward. Silk, spices, and paper moved west, while glass, gold, and wool moved east. However, goods were not the only things that travelled. Religions, languages, and ideas moved along the same routes, and so did diseases. Buddhism, for instance, spread from India into China, carried by monks who joined the caravans. Cities such as Samarkand grew rich because they stood at the crossroads where routes met. When sea travel became faster and cheaper in the fifteenth century, the overland routes slowly declined. Yet historians agree that the exchange of knowledge along the Silk Road did more to shape the world than any single cargo of silk.', array['sm2-reading-5','sm2-reading-6','sm2-reading-7','sm2-reading-8']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm2-3', 'The Story of the Bicycle', 'The first bicycle appeared in Germany in 1817. Karl von Drais built a wooden machine with two wheels, but it had no pedals; riders pushed along the ground with their feet. Fifty years later, French inventors attached pedals to the front wheel, and the "boneshaker" got its name from the rough ride its iron wheels produced. The next design, the high-wheeler, had an enormous front wheel that made it faster but dangerous, because riders sat high above the ground and fell forward if they hit a stone. In the 1880s the "safety bicycle" solved the problem. It had two wheels of equal size and a chain that drove the back wheel, so the rider sat low and stable. Rubber tyres filled with air arrived soon afterwards and made the ride much smoother. Because bicycles were cheap, they gave ordinary people, especially women, a new freedom to travel without depending on horses or trains.', array['sm2-reading-9','sm2-reading-10','sm2-reading-11','sm2-reading-12']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm2-4', 'Why Young People Should Volunteer', 'Many teenagers believe that volunteering is something to do only when school work is finished. I disagree. Giving a few hours each week to a local charity or neighbourhood project is among the best investments a young person can make. First, volunteers learn practical skills such as teamwork, planning, and communication, which no textbook can fully teach. Second, helping others builds confidence, because the results are visible immediately. Third, many universities and employers notice applicants who have served their communities, so volunteering can open doors later. Critics say that students are already too busy, and they have a point: nobody should sacrifice sleep or study. Nevertheless, even one afternoon a week is enough to make a difference. Schools should therefore make time for community service instead of treating it as an optional extra.', array['sm2-reading-13','sm2-reading-14','sm2-reading-15','sm2-reading-16']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm2-5', 'The Date Palm in Arabian Life', 'The date palm has been part of life in Arabia for thousands of years. Its fruit is rich in sugar and fibre, which gave travellers energy during long journeys across the desert. Nothing from the tree is wasted: the leaves are woven into baskets and mats, the trunk is used as timber for roofs, and even the date stones are ground and fed to animals. A mature tree can produce more than a hundred kilograms of fruit each year, although it takes several years after planting to bear its first crop. Date palms also help the environment. Their shade protects smaller crops from the harsh sun, and their roots hold the soil together in oases. Today Saudi Arabia is one of the world''s largest producers of dates, with hundreds of varieties grown across the kingdom. Each variety differs in colour, size and sweetness, and many families still have a favourite that they serve to guests.', array['sm2-reading-17','sm2-reading-18','sm2-reading-19','sm2-reading-20']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm2-6', 'How Vaccines Work', 'A vaccine teaches the body to defend itself without making a person ill. It contains a harmless piece or a weakened form of a germ, which is enough for the immune system to recognise as an intruder. The body responds by producing antibodies, special proteins that attach to the germ and help destroy it. Crucially, the immune system also creates memory cells that remain in the blood for years. If the real germ ever enters the body, these cells recognise it quickly and trigger a fast response, often before any symptoms appear. Some vaccines need booster doses because the memory fades over time. When most people in a community are vaccinated, the disease has difficulty spreading, and this protects even those who cannot be vaccinated, such as newborn babies. Doctors call this "community immunity". Smallpox, which once killed millions, was completely wiped out in 1980 through worldwide vaccination.', array['sm2-reading-21','sm2-reading-22','sm2-reading-23','sm2-reading-24']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm2-7', 'Why the World Has Time Zones', 'Before the late nineteenth century, every town set its clocks by the sun, so noon in one city could differ from noon in a city only fifty kilometres away. This caused little trouble until railways appeared. Timetables became confusing, and travellers sometimes missed trains simply because two stations kept different times. In 1884, delegates from twenty-five countries met in Washington and agreed to divide the world into twenty-four time zones, each roughly fifteen degrees wide, with Greenwich in London as the starting point. Most countries gradually adopted the system, although some chose unusual arrangements. China, which is geographically as wide as five zones, uses a single time across the whole country. Saudi Arabia keeps the same time everywhere, three hours ahead of Greenwich. Although the system is not perfect, it allows airlines, banks and families in different countries to plan their days with confidence.', array['sm2-reading-25','sm2-reading-26','sm2-reading-27','sm2-reading-28']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm2-8', 'Camels: Survivors of the Desert', 'Camels have lived in the deserts of Arabia for thousands of years, and their bodies are well designed for the harsh conditions. Contrary to popular belief, a camel''s hump does not store water; it stores fat, which the animal can use as energy when food is scarce. A camel can drink up to a hundred litres of water in only a few minutes, and it can then travel for days without drinking. Its thick eyelashes and closable nostrils keep out blowing sand, and wide feet stop it from sinking into soft ground. Camels also lose very little water through sweat, because their body temperature is allowed to rise during the day before cooling at night. For centuries Bedouin families depended on camels for transport, milk and wool. Although cars have replaced them for most journeys, camels remain a proud symbol of the region, and they are still raced and bred across the kingdom.', array['sm2-reading-29','sm2-reading-30','sm2-reading-31','sm2-reading-32']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm2-9', 'The Printing Press', 'Before the fifteenth century, books were copied by hand, a slow process that could take a monk many months to finish a single volume. As a result, books were extremely expensive, and only the church and the very rich owned them. Around 1440, a German goldsmith named Johannes Gutenberg developed a printing press that used movable metal letters. The letters could be arranged into a page, inked, pressed onto paper, and then rearranged for the next page. Within fifty years, printing presses operated in more than two hundred European cities, and millions of books had been produced. Prices fell sharply, so ordinary families could afford them. Literacy rates rose, new ideas travelled faster, and scientists could share discoveries and correct each other''s mistakes. Some rulers feared the new technology because they could no longer control what people read. Historians often rank the printing press among the most influential inventions in history.', array['sm2-reading-33','sm2-reading-34','sm2-reading-35','sm2-reading-36']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm2-10', 'The Rise of Solar Power', 'Solar panels convert sunlight directly into electricity using thin layers of silicon. When light strikes the silicon, it knocks electrons loose, and these electrons flow through wires as an electric current. Because the process has no moving parts and burns no fuel, a panel can work quietly for twenty-five years or more. Twenty years ago, solar power was too expensive for most people, but improved manufacturing has reduced the price of panels by roughly ninety percent. The Arabian Peninsula, with its clear skies and long days, receives some of the strongest sunlight on Earth, which makes it an ideal place for large solar farms. Saudi Arabia has announced projects capable of powering millions of homes. Nevertheless, solar power has limits. Panels produce nothing at night, so engineers are developing better batteries to store the extra energy created during the day. Until storage becomes cheaper, solar power will probably work alongside other energy sources rather than replace them.', array['sm2-reading-37','sm2-reading-38','sm2-reading-39','sm2-reading-40']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm3-1', 'Why the Moon Seems to Change Shape', 'The Moon does not produce any light of its own. What we see is sunlight bouncing off its surface. Because the Moon travels around the Earth once every twenty-nine and a half days or so, the part of its lit side that faces us keeps changing. When the Moon sits between the Earth and the Sun, its lit side faces away from us, and we see almost nothing; this is called the new moon. A week later, we see half of the lit side, and a week after that the whole face shines as a full moon. Then the cycle runs backwards until the Moon disappears again. Many people believe that the phases are caused by the Earth''s shadow falling on the Moon, but this is a mistake. The Earth''s shadow only covers the Moon during a lunar eclipse, which is rare. For centuries, the cycle of the Moon has guided calendars, and the Islamic calendar still begins each month with the sighting of the new crescent.', array['sm3-reading-1','sm3-reading-2','sm3-reading-3','sm3-reading-4']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm3-2', 'How Aeroplanes Stay in the Air', 'An aeroplane weighs many tonnes, yet it rises into the sky because of its wings. A wing is curved on top and flatter underneath. As the plane rushes forward, the wing''s shape and slight tilt guide the air downward, and the pressure above the wing becomes lower than the pressure below it. The higher pressure beneath pushes the wing upward. This upward force is called lift. To move forward fast enough to create lift, a plane needs thrust, which is produced by powerful engines. Pilots control the plane by adjusting small moving parts on the wings and tail, which change the way air flows around them. Contrary to what many people think, the engines do not hold the aeroplane up; if they stop in flight, a plane can still glide for a long distance. Because lift depends on speed, planes need long runways to build up enough speed before take-off.', array['sm3-reading-5','sm3-reading-6','sm3-reading-7','sm3-reading-8']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm3-3', 'Understanding Stress', 'Stress is the body''s natural reaction to pressure. When we face a difficult exam or a sudden danger, the brain releases chemicals that make the heart beat faster and the muscles tighten. In short bursts this response is helpful, because it gives us extra energy and focus. The problem begins when stress continues for weeks or months. Long-term stress can cause headaches, poor sleep, and difficulty concentrating, and it may weaken the body''s ability to fight illness. Doctors recommend several simple ways to manage it. Regular exercise burns off the extra energy, while a fixed sleeping routine helps the body recover. Talking to a friend or family member can also lighten the burden, since a problem shared often feels smaller. Students in particular should break large tasks into small steps instead of leaving everything to the last night. Although stress cannot be removed from life completely, learning to control it can protect both health and performance.', array['sm3-reading-9','sm3-reading-10','sm3-reading-11','sm3-reading-12']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm3-4', 'Staying Safe Online', 'Every day, millions of people log in to websites that hold their private information, so a strong password is the first line of defence. A good password is long, at least twelve characters, and mixes letters, numbers and symbols. It should not contain personal details such as a birthday or a pet''s name, because attackers try these first. Experts also warn against using the same password on several sites; if one site is hacked, criminals can open every other account. Because remembering many complicated passwords is difficult, a password manager, a program that stores them safely, can help. Another important protection is two-step verification, which sends a code to your phone before allowing access. Finally, be careful with messages that ask you to click a link and enter your details. These "phishing" messages look real, but their purpose is to steal your information. When in doubt, go to the website directly instead.', array['sm3-reading-13','sm3-reading-14','sm3-reading-15','sm3-reading-16']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm3-5', 'Diriyah: Birthplace of the Saudi State', 'Diriyah, on the edge of Riyadh, is often called the birthplace of the Saudi state. In 1727, Imam Muhammad bin Saud became its ruler, and from here the first Saudi state grew across much of the Arabian Peninsula. The city''s most famous district, At-Turaif, was built in the traditional Najdi style, using mud brick that keeps interiors cool in the summer heat. Thick walls, small windows and shaded courtyards were all designed to protect people from the sun. In 2010, UNESCO added At-Turaif to its list of World Heritage Sites, recognising its value for all humanity. Today, visitors can walk through restored palaces, learn about the history of the kingdom in its museums, and enjoy traditional food in the old streets. The restoration project aims to protect the buildings while welcoming large numbers of visitors, so that future generations can understand where their country began.', array['sm3-reading-17','sm3-reading-18','sm3-reading-19','sm3-reading-20']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm3-6', 'The Water Cycle', 'The water on Earth is constantly moving in a cycle that has no beginning or end. The Sun heats the surface of oceans, lakes and rivers, and some of the water turns into an invisible gas called water vapour. This process is known as evaporation. Plants add to the supply by releasing water through their leaves. As the vapour rises, the air around it becomes colder, and the vapour condenses into tiny drops that gather to form clouds. When the drops grow heavy, they fall as rain, snow or hail. Some of this water flows into rivers and returns to the sea, while some sinks into the ground and is stored as groundwater. In dry countries such as Saudi Arabia, this underground water is extremely precious, because rain is rare and the water takes thousands of years to be replaced once it is used.', array['sm3-reading-21','sm3-reading-22','sm3-reading-23','sm3-reading-24']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm3-7', 'Sea Turtles in Danger', 'Sea turtles have swum in the world''s oceans for more than a hundred million years, but today six of the seven species are considered threatened. Female turtles return to the same beach where they were born to lay their eggs, often travelling thousands of kilometres to reach it. A mother digs a hole in the sand, lays about a hundred eggs, covers them, and returns to the sea. After about two months, the baby turtles hatch at night and crawl toward the brightest horizon, which is normally the moonlit sea. Unfortunately, artificial lights on modern beaches confuse them, and many head inland instead and die. Plastic bags are another danger, because turtles mistake them for jellyfish and eat them. Conservation groups protect nests with cages, switch off lights near beaches during hatching season, and teach visitors not to leave rubbish behind. Their work shows that small changes in human behaviour can save an ancient species.', array['sm3-reading-25','sm3-reading-26','sm3-reading-27','sm3-reading-28']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm3-8', 'The Rise of Online Shopping', 'Twenty years ago, most people bought clothes, books and electronics in physical shops. Today, a growing number of customers prefer to click a button and wait for a delivery. The main reason is convenience: shoppers can compare prices from home at any hour, read reviews written by other buyers, and return items if they are not satisfied. For small businesses, the internet offers a way to reach customers in distant cities without paying for an expensive shop. However, online shopping has disadvantages. Buyers cannot touch or try products before paying, and some items arrive different from the pictures. There is also growing concern about the packaging waste created by millions of deliveries. Many experts expect traditional shops to survive by offering something websites cannot, such as personal advice and a chance to experience products directly. In the future, the most successful companies will probably combine both methods.', array['sm3-reading-29','sm3-reading-30','sm3-reading-31','sm3-reading-32']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm3-9', 'Marie Curie: A Scientist Against the Odds', 'Marie Curie was born in Warsaw in 1867. As a young woman she could not study at her local university, because women were not admitted, so she saved money for years and moved to Paris. There she studied physics and mathematics, working long hours in a small, cold room. She later married Pierre Curie, a fellow scientist, and together they studied the strange rays given off by certain minerals. Marie named this property radioactivity and discovered two new elements, polonium and radium. In 1903 she shared the Nobel Prize in Physics, and in 1911 she won the Nobel Prize in Chemistry on her own, becoming the first person to win in two different sciences. During the First World War she helped build mobile X-ray units that were driven close to the battlefields, and these units helped doctors treat wounded soldiers. She died in 1934, probably as a result of her long exposure to radiation, whose dangers were not understood at the time.', array['sm3-reading-33','sm3-reading-34','sm3-reading-35','sm3-reading-36']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm3-10', 'Why Recycling Matters', 'Every year, people throw away billions of tonnes of rubbish, and much of it ends up in landfills, where it can take decades or even centuries to break down. Recycling reduces this problem by turning old materials into new products. Paper, for example, can be pulped and made into fresh sheets several times, which saves trees and uses less water and energy than producing paper from wood. Metals such as aluminium are even more valuable, because they can be melted and reused again and again without losing quality. Plastic is more difficult, since many types cannot be mixed, and some can only be recycled once. For this reason, experts say that recycling alone is not enough; we must also reduce the amount we use and reuse what we already have. A simple habit, such as carrying a cloth bag to the shop, prevents hundreds of plastic bags from entering the environment over a lifetime.', array['sm3-reading-37','sm3-reading-38','sm3-reading-39','sm3-reading-40']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm4-1', 'How Tides Work', 'Anyone who has spent a day at the beach has seen the sea rise and fall. These movements are called tides, and they are caused mainly by the Moon. The Moon''s gravity pulls on the Earth, and because water can move freely, the oceans bulge slightly toward the Moon. As the Earth turns, a given coast passes through these bulges and experiences high tide roughly twice a day, with low tide between them. The Sun also pulls on the oceans, although its effect is smaller because it is much farther away. When the Sun, Moon and Earth line up, at full moon and new moon, their combined pull creates especially large tides known as spring tides. Fishermen and sailors have always studied tide tables, because a boat can be trapped on the sand at low tide. Today, engineers are even building power stations that use the moving water to generate electricity without burning fuel.', array['sm4-reading-1','sm4-reading-2','sm4-reading-3','sm4-reading-4']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm4-2', 'The Human Heart', 'The heart is a muscle about the size of a fist, and it never rests. Beating around seventy times a minute, it pumps blood through a network of vessels that would stretch for tens of thousands of kilometres if laid end to end. The right side of the heart sends blood to the lungs, where it picks up oxygen. The left side then pumps this oxygen-rich blood to the rest of the body. Four valves act like one-way doors, ensuring that blood flows in the correct direction. A healthy heart can adapt to demands: during exercise it beats faster so that the muscles receive more oxygen. Doctors say that the best way to protect it is a combination of regular exercise, a diet low in salt and fat, and avoiding smoking. Small habits such as walking daily may add years to a person''s life.', array['sm4-reading-5','sm4-reading-6','sm4-reading-7','sm4-reading-8']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm4-3', 'The Haramain High-Speed Railway', 'The Haramain High-Speed Railway links the holy cities of Makkah and Madinah, with stops at Jeddah and King Abdullah Economic City. Opened in 2018, the line is about 450 kilometres long, and its trains travel at speeds of up to 300 kilometres per hour. Before the railway, the journey between the two cities by road took around five hours; by train it takes about two and a half. The stations were designed with the needs of pilgrims in mind, with wide halls, clear signs in several languages, and prayer rooms. Because the trains run on electricity, they produce less pollution than the cars and buses that would otherwise carry the same passengers. The project also created thousands of jobs during construction. Officials hope that the railway will encourage more visitors to see the region, and that it will serve as a model for future transport projects in the kingdom.', array['sm4-reading-9','sm4-reading-10','sm4-reading-11','sm4-reading-12']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm4-4', 'Why Breakfast Matters', 'Many students skip breakfast because they are in a hurry, but nutrition experts say this is a mistake. After a night without food, the body''s energy supply is low, and a meal in the morning refills it. Studies have found that students who eat breakfast usually concentrate better in class and remember more of what they learn. A good breakfast does not need to be complicated: whole-grain bread, eggs, yoghurt, fruit and milk provide energy that lasts for hours, while sugary cereals and pastries give a quick boost that fades fast and leaves people hungry again. Some people argue that they simply do not feel hungry in the morning. In that case, a small snack such as a banana is better than nothing. The writer concludes that a few minutes at breakfast is among the cheapest ways to improve both health and school results.', array['sm4-reading-13','sm4-reading-14','sm4-reading-15','sm4-reading-16']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm4-5', 'The Great Pyramid of Giza', 'The Great Pyramid of Giza was built around 2500 BCE as the tomb of the pharaoh Khufu. It is made of more than two million blocks of stone, some weighing as much as a small truck, and for about 3,800 years it was the tallest structure on Earth. Historians once believed that the pyramid was built by slaves, but discoveries of workers'' villages near the site suggest that the builders were paid labourers who were fed well and given medical care. The workers probably moved the heavy blocks on wooden sledges, pouring water on the sand in front of them to make it easier to drag. When it was finished, the pyramid was covered with smooth white limestone that shone in the sun. Most of this outer layer has since been removed or has fallen away, which is why the monument looks rough today.', array['sm4-reading-17','sm4-reading-18','sm4-reading-19','sm4-reading-20']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm4-6', 'How Glass Is Made', 'Glass has been made for more than four thousand years, but the basic recipe has hardly changed. The main ingredient is sand, which is mixed with soda ash and limestone and heated in a furnace to about 1,500 degrees Celsius until it melts into a glowing liquid. In the past, craftsmen shaped this liquid by blowing air through a long pipe, a technique that is still used for decorative glassware. Modern factories prefer a different method: the molten glass is poured onto a bath of melted tin, where it spreads out evenly and forms perfectly flat sheets for windows. One of the most useful qualities of glass is that it can be recycled again and again without losing its quality, so old bottles are crushed and melted to make new ones, which saves both energy and raw materials.', array['sm4-reading-21','sm4-reading-22','sm4-reading-23','sm4-reading-24']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm4-7', 'Emperor Penguins', 'Emperor penguins live in Antarctica, where winter temperatures can fall below minus forty degrees Celsius and winds blow at more than a hundred kilometres per hour. To survive, they gather in huge groups called huddles, standing so close together that they share their body heat. Penguins on the windy outside edge slowly move toward the warmer centre, while those in the middle take their turn at the edge, so that no single bird suffers the cold for long. The mother lays one egg and goes to sea to feed, while the father balances the egg on his feet, under a warm fold of skin, for about two months without eating. When the chick hatches, the mother returns with food. Scientists admire this cooperation, which shows how a species can survive extreme conditions by working as a team.', array['sm4-reading-25','sm4-reading-26','sm4-reading-27','sm4-reading-28']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm4-8', 'Teenagers and Social Media', 'Few teenagers can imagine life without social media, and there is no doubt that it has benefits. It allows them to keep in touch with relatives abroad, share ideas with people who have the same interests, and even learn new skills from online videos. However, I believe that many young people spend far too long on their screens. Studies suggest that those who check their phones constantly sleep less and find it harder to concentrate on homework. Another problem is comparison: when teenagers see only the best moments of other people''s lives, they may feel that their own lives are boring. I am not suggesting that social media should be banned. Instead, families should agree on simple rules, such as switching off phones during meals and an hour before bedtime. With these limits, technology can remain a useful tool rather than a harmful habit.', array['sm4-reading-29','sm4-reading-30','sm4-reading-31','sm4-reading-32']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm4-9', 'The Discovery of Penicillin', 'In 1928, the Scottish scientist Alexander Fleming returned from a holiday to his untidy laboratory in London and noticed something strange. A dish of bacteria he had forgotten to clean was covered with mould, and around the mould the bacteria had died. Fleming realised that the mould was producing a substance that could kill harmful germs, and he named it penicillin. However, he was unable to produce enough of the substance to treat patients. It was more than ten years later that two other scientists, Howard Florey and Ernst Chain, found a way to make penicillin in large quantities, just in time to treat wounded soldiers in the Second World War. In 1945, all three men shared the Nobel Prize. Penicillin was the first of many antibiotics that have since saved millions of lives.', array['sm4-reading-33','sm4-reading-34','sm4-reading-35','sm4-reading-36']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm4-10', 'Fresh Water from the Sea', 'Saudi Arabia has very little rainfall and no permanent rivers, so it relies heavily on desalination, the process of removing salt from seawater. The kingdom produces more desalinated water than any other country in the world, and many cities, including Riyadh, receive part of their drinking water from plants on the coast. There are two main methods. In thermal plants, seawater is heated until it turns into vapour, leaving the salt behind; the vapour is then cooled into fresh water. In reverse osmosis plants, powerful pumps push seawater through very fine filters that block the salt. Reverse osmosis uses less energy, so most new plants use it. Desalination is expensive, however, and the salty water left over must be returned carefully to the sea so that it does not harm marine life. For this reason, engineers are working on cheaper, cleaner technology, including plants that run on solar power.', array['sm4-reading-37','sm4-reading-38','sm4-reading-39','sm4-reading-40']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm5-1', 'The Empty Quarter', 'The Empty Quarter, or Rub'' al Khali, is the largest continuous sand desert in the world. It covers about 650,000 square kilometres across Saudi Arabia, Oman, the United Arab Emirates and Yemen, an area larger than France. Giant dunes, some more than 250 metres high, roll across the landscape like frozen waves. Rain may not fall for years, and summer temperatures often pass 50 degrees Celsius, yet life survives. Small animals such as sand cats and desert lizards hide in burrows by day and hunt at night, while tough plants send their roots deep to find hidden water. For centuries, only Bedouin guides who knew the stars and the wind could cross the desert safely. Today, scientists use satellites to study it, and the oil industry has built roads and camps in parts of the sand. Despite this, most of the Empty Quarter remains almost untouched.', array['sm5-reading-1','sm5-reading-2','sm5-reading-3','sm5-reading-4']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm5-2', 'A Short History of Clocks', 'Long before mechanical clocks, people measured time with the sun. The ancient Egyptians used tall stone pillars whose shadows moved during the day, and later sundials divided the day into hours. Sundials were useless at night or on cloudy days, so inventors looked for other methods. Water clocks measured time by the steady flow of water from one container to another, while candles marked hours as they burned down. In the fourteenth century, European craftsmen built the first mechanical clocks driven by falling weights; they were enormous and were placed in church towers. The big breakthrough came in 1656, when the Dutch scientist Christiaan Huygens used a swinging pendulum to regulate the movement, making clocks far more accurate. Smaller springs later allowed people to carry watches in their pockets. Today, atomic clocks lose less than a second in millions of years.', array['sm5-reading-5','sm5-reading-6','sm5-reading-7','sm5-reading-8']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm5-3', 'The Intelligence of Elephants', 'Elephants are the largest land animals on Earth, and they are also among the most intelligent. An African elephant''s brain weighs about five kilograms, several times heavier than a human brain, and it controls an enormous range of skills. Elephants live in family groups led by the oldest female, called the matriarch. She remembers where water and food can be found during droughts, and she leads the herd there, sometimes over hundreds of kilometres. Researchers have also observed elephants gathering around an animal that has died, touching the body gently with their trunks, which suggests that they feel grief. In recent decades, hunting for ivory has greatly reduced their numbers. Many countries now protect them, and tourists who watch elephants in national parks help to pay for this protection. Scientists hope that, with continued care, these remarkable animals will survive for future generations.', array['sm5-reading-9','sm5-reading-10','sm5-reading-11','sm5-reading-12']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm5-4', 'The Power of Reading for Pleasure', 'Reading for pleasure is one of the best habits a student can develop. First, it widens vocabulary: people who read regularly meet thousands of new words in context, and they learn their meaning without memorising lists. Second, it improves writing, because readers absorb how good authors build sentences and paragraphs. Third, reading is relaxing; just six minutes with a book can lower stress levels, according to one study. Fourth, it trains concentration, a skill that is becoming rarer in a world of constant notifications. Teachers often recommend starting with books on subjects you already enjoy, such as football, space or cooking, and choosing a level that is slightly easy. If a book is too difficult, you will lose interest and stop. Even twenty minutes before bed is enough. Over a year, this small habit adds up to many books and a noticeably stronger command of the language.', array['sm5-reading-13','sm5-reading-14','sm5-reading-15','sm5-reading-16']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm5-5', 'Mount Everest', 'Mount Everest, on the border between Nepal and Tibet, is the highest mountain above sea level at 8,849 metres. It is named after Sir George Everest, a British surveyor, although local people have long used the names Sagarmatha in Nepali and Chomolungma in Tibetan. In 1953, Edmund Hillary of New Zealand and the Sherpa guide Tenzing Norgay became the first climbers confirmed to reach the summit. Since then, thousands have followed. Climbers face freezing temperatures, powerful winds and thin air, which contains only about a third of the oxygen found at sea level, so most carry bottled oxygen. Sherpa guides, who live in the region and are used to altitude, carry equipment and fix ropes, but their work is dangerous and often unrecognised. In recent years, the large number of climbers has also left rubbish on the slopes, and clean-up expeditions now collect tonnes of waste.', array['sm5-reading-17','sm5-reading-18','sm5-reading-19','sm5-reading-20']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm5-6', 'Al-Balad: The Historic Heart of Jeddah', 'Al-Balad, the historic centre of Jeddah, has been a trading port on the Red Sea for more than a thousand years. Pilgrims arriving by ship passed through its gates on their way to Makkah, and merchants from India, Egypt and Africa filled its markets with spices, cloth and coffee. The district is famous for its tall houses built from coral stone, a light material cut from the sea. These buildings have wooden balconies called rawasheen, whose carved screens let sea breezes inside while keeping the rooms private and shaded. In 2014, UNESCO listed Al-Balad as a World Heritage Site. Since then, a restoration programme has repaired many damaged houses, and old shops now sell traditional crafts to visitors. Local people hope that this attention will protect the area from neglect and fires, which have destroyed some historic buildings in the past.', array['sm5-reading-21','sm5-reading-22','sm5-reading-23','sm5-reading-24']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm5-7', 'How Bridges Carry Weight', 'A bridge must support its own weight and the weight of everything that crosses it. Engineers choose a design according to the distance it must cover. The simplest type, the beam bridge, is a flat structure resting on supports at each end; it is cheap but suitable only for short distances. An arch bridge curves upward and pushes the load outward and downward into the ground at both ends, which allows it to span longer distances using stone or concrete. For very long crossings, engineers build suspension bridges, in which a roadway hangs from strong steel cables strung between tall towers. The Golden Gate Bridge in San Francisco is a famous example. Cable-stayed bridges are similar, but the cables run directly from the towers to the road. Whatever the type, designers must also consider wind, earthquakes and the effect of heavy traffic over many decades.', array['sm5-reading-25','sm5-reading-26','sm5-reading-27','sm5-reading-28']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm5-8', 'The Hidden Cost of Fast Fashion', 'In the past, people bought clothes a few times a year and expected them to last. Today, "fast fashion" companies produce cheap clothes in huge quantities and put new styles in shops every few weeks. Customers enjoy the low prices and the variety, but the system has serious costs. Making a single cotton shirt can use thousands of litres of water, and many synthetic fabrics release tiny plastic fibres when washed, which end up in rivers and oceans. Because the clothes are not made to last, millions of tonnes are thrown away each year. I think shoppers have more power than they realise. If we buy fewer items, choose better quality, repair what we own and pass unwanted clothes to others, the demand for endless new products will fall. Fashion should be about enjoying what we wear, not about replacing it constantly.', array['sm5-reading-29','sm5-reading-30','sm5-reading-31','sm5-reading-32']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm5-9', 'Ibn Battuta: The Great Traveller', 'Ibn Battuta was born in Tangier, Morocco, in 1304 into a family of legal scholars. At the age of twenty-one he left home to perform Hajj in Makkah, intending to return within a few years. Instead, he spent almost thirty years travelling, covering around 120,000 kilometres across North Africa, the Middle East, India, China and parts of Africa south of the Sahara. He travelled by camel, ship and on foot, joined caravans for protection, and survived shipwrecks and robbers. Wherever he went, he met rulers and scholars, and he often worked as a judge, because of his religious education. When he finally returned home, the ruler of Morocco asked him to dictate an account of his journeys. This book, the Rihla, is still read today and is valuable to historians because it describes cities and customs that were rarely recorded by other writers.', array['sm5-reading-33','sm5-reading-34','sm5-reading-35','sm5-reading-36']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.reading_passages (id, title, body, question_ids) values ('passage-sm5-10', 'Artificial Intelligence in Daily Life', 'Artificial intelligence, or AI, is no longer something found only in science fiction. Every time a phone suggests the next word in a message, or a map application chooses the fastest route, AI is at work. These systems learn by studying enormous amounts of data and finding patterns that humans might miss. In medicine, AI programs can examine thousands of X-ray images and help doctors spot early signs of disease. In schools, some applications adapt exercises to a student''s level, giving extra practice where it is needed. However, AI also raises concerns. Systems can make mistakes, and they may be unfair if the data they learn from is incomplete. There are also worries about privacy, because AI often relies on personal information. Experts therefore argue that people should treat AI as a helpful assistant, check important results themselves, and support clear rules about how the technology may be used.', array['sm5-reading-37','sm5-reading-38','sm5-reading-39','sm5-reading-40']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, question_ids = excluded.question_ids;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-1', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'Neither of the students ___ finished the assignment yet.', '[{"id":"a","text":"has"},{"id":"b","text":"have"},{"id":"c","text":"having"},{"id":"d","text":"had been"}]'::jsonb, 'a', '"Neither of" takes a singular verb, so "has" is correct, not "have".', 'medium', array['subject-verb-agreement']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-2', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'By the time she arrived, the meeting ___ already ___.', '[{"id":"a","text":"has / started"},{"id":"b","text":"had / started"},{"id":"c","text":"was / starting"},{"id":"d","text":"have / start"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'An action completed before another past action uses the past perfect: "had started".', 'medium', array['tenses','past-perfect']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-3', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'The report was written ___ the marketing team, not the finance department.', '[{"id":"a","text":"from"},{"id":"b","text":"with"},{"id":"c","text":"by"},{"id":"d","text":"for"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'The passive voice agent is introduced with "by": "written by the marketing team".', 'easy', array['prepositions','passive-voice']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-4', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'If the company ___ its prices, it would attract more customers.', '[{"id":"a","text":"lowers"},{"id":"b","text":"will lower"},{"id":"c","text":"had lowered"},{"id":"d","text":"lowered"}]'::jsonb, 'd', 'Second conditional (hypothetical present/future): "If + past simple, would + base verb".', 'medium', array['conditionals']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-5', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'This is the engineer ___ design won the award.', '[{"id":"a","text":"who"},{"id":"b","text":"whose"},{"id":"c","text":"which"},{"id":"d","text":"whom"}]'::jsonb, 'b', '"Whose" shows possession (the engineer''s design), which the other relative pronouns don''t.', 'medium', array['relative-clauses']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-6', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'She is ___ than her older brother at solving logic puzzles.', '[{"id":"a","text":"more good"},{"id":"b","text":"best"},{"id":"c","text":"better"},{"id":"d","text":"the better"}]'::jsonb, 'c', '"Good" has an irregular comparative form: "better", not "more good".', 'easy', array['comparatives']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-7', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'The number of applicants ___ increased significantly this year.', '[{"id":"a","text":"have"},{"id":"b","text":"are"},{"id":"c","text":"were"},{"id":"d","text":"has"}]'::jsonb, 'd', '"The number of" is treated as singular ("a number of" is plural, but "the number of" is not).', 'hard', array['subject-verb-agreement']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-8', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'I would rather you ___ me before making a decision.', '[{"id":"a","text":"consulted"},{"id":"b","text":"consult"},{"id":"c","text":"will consult"},{"id":"d","text":"consulting"}]'::jsonb, 'a', '"Would rather + subject + past simple" expresses a present/future wish about someone else''s action.', 'hard', array['subjunctive']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-9', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'She enjoys ___ new software before recommending it to clients.', '[{"id":"a","text":"to try"},{"id":"b","text":"try"},{"id":"c","text":"trying"},{"id":"d","text":"tried"}]'::jsonb, 'c', '"Enjoy" is always followed by a gerund ("enjoy doing"), never a to-infinitive.', 'easy', array['gerunds-infinitives']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-10', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'He isn''t answering his phone, and I am almost certain that he ___ be in a meeting.', '[{"id":"a","text":"might"},{"id":"b","text":"should"},{"id":"c","text":"needn''t"},{"id":"d","text":"must"}]'::jsonb, 'd', '"Must" expresses a strong logical deduction based on available evidence — the most likely explanation.', 'medium', array['modals','deduction']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-11', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'The new policy ___ by the board next week.', '[{"id":"a","text":"will be announced"},{"id":"b","text":"will announce"},{"id":"c","text":"is announcing"},{"id":"d","text":"announced"}]'::jsonb, 'a', 'The policy doesn''t announce itself — it receives the action, so the passive future ("will be announced") is correct.', 'medium', array['passive-voice','tenses']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-12', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, '___ Nile is the longest river in Africa.', '[{"id":"a","text":"A"},{"id":"b","text":"The"},{"id":"c","text":"An"},{"id":"d","text":"(no article)"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'Names of rivers take the definite article: "the Nile", "the Amazon".', 'easy', array['articles']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-13', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'The workshop focused on writing clearly, editing efficiently, and ___ persuasively.', '[{"id":"a","text":"to speak"},{"id":"b","text":"spoke"},{"id":"c","text":"speak"},{"id":"d","text":"speaking"}]'::jsonb, 'd', 'A list joined by "and" must use parallel grammatical forms — "writing, editing, speaking" are all gerunds.', 'medium', array['parallel-structure']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-14', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'She said that she ___ the report by Friday.', '[{"id":"a","text":"would finish"},{"id":"b","text":"will finish"},{"id":"c","text":"finishes"},{"id":"d","text":"has finished"}]'::jsonb, 'a', 'Reported speech shifts "will" back to "would" when the reporting verb ("said") is in the past tense.', 'medium', array['reported-speech']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-15', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'This model is not ___ efficient as the previous one.', '[{"id":"a","text":"such"},{"id":"b","text":"as"},{"id":"c","text":"more"},{"id":"d","text":"very"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'Equal comparisons use "as + adjective + as"; the negative form is "not as...as".', 'medium', array['comparatives']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-16', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'The company is responsible ___ maintaining workplace safety.', '[{"id":"a","text":"of"},{"id":"b","text":"with"},{"id":"c","text":"for"},{"id":"d","text":"about"}]'::jsonb, 'c', '"Responsible" collocates with "for": "responsible for (doing) something".', 'easy', array['prepositions','collocations']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-17', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'I need to have my car ___ before the trip.', '[{"id":"a","text":"repaired"},{"id":"b","text":"repair"},{"id":"c","text":"repairing"},{"id":"d","text":"to repair"}]'::jsonb, 'a', 'The causative "have something done" uses a past participle: "have my car repaired".', 'medium', array['causative']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-18', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'Rarely ___ such a generous offer from a competitor.', '[{"id":"a","text":"we see"},{"id":"b","text":"do we see"},{"id":"c","text":"we do see"},{"id":"d","text":"did we saw"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'Negative adverbs like "rarely" at the start of a sentence require inversion: "Rarely do we see...".', 'hard', array['inversion']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-19', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'You''ve already finished the report, ___?', '[{"id":"a","text":"hasn''t it"},{"id":"b","text":"didn''t you"},{"id":"c","text":"haven''t you"},{"id":"d","text":"aren''t you"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'Question tags match the auxiliary and subject of the main clause: "You''ve finished" → "haven''t you".', 'medium', array['question-tags']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-20', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, '___ or not she will accept the job offer remains uncertain.', '[{"id":"a","text":"What"},{"id":"b","text":"That"},{"id":"c","text":"If"},{"id":"d","text":"Whether"}]'::jsonb, 'd', '"Whether" can introduce a noun clause in subject position; "if" normally cannot be used this way.', 'hard', array['noun-clauses']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-21', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'When I was a child, I ___ live in a small village near the coast.', '[{"id":"a","text":"would"},{"id":"b","text":"used to"},{"id":"c","text":"was used to"},{"id":"d","text":"use to"}]'::jsonb, 'b', '"Used to" describes a past state (living somewhere); "would" is only for repeated past actions, not states.', 'medium', array['used-to-would']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-22', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'The meeting had to be ___ due to unexpected bad weather.', '[{"id":"a","text":"called on"},{"id":"b","text":"called in"},{"id":"c","text":"called off"},{"id":"d","text":"called up"}]'::jsonb, 'c', '"Called off" is a phrasal verb meaning cancelled.', 'easy', array['phrasal-verbs']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-23', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'This is ___ most efficient method we''ve tested so far.', '[{"id":"a","text":"a"},{"id":"b","text":"an"},{"id":"c","text":"(no article)"},{"id":"d","text":"the"}]'::jsonb, 'd', 'Superlative forms take the definite article: "the most efficient".', 'easy', array['articles','superlatives']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-24', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'She''s very good ___ solving complex problems quickly.', '[{"id":"a","text":"at"},{"id":"b","text":"in"},{"id":"c","text":"on"},{"id":"d","text":"for"}]'::jsonb, 'a', '"Good" collocates with "at": "good at (doing) something".', 'easy', array['prepositions','collocations']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-25', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'A number of students ___ registered for the evening seminar.', '[{"id":"a","text":"has"},{"id":"b","text":"is"},{"id":"c","text":"have"},{"id":"d","text":"was"}]'::jsonb, 'c', '"A number of" refers to several individual items and takes a plural verb — the opposite of "the number of", which is singular.', 'hard', array['subject-verb-agreement']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-26', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, '___ the heavy rain, the outdoor festival continued as planned.', '[{"id":"a","text":"Although"},{"id":"b","text":"In spite"},{"id":"c","text":"Even though"},{"id":"d","text":"Despite"}]'::jsonb, 'd', '"Despite" is a preposition that takes a noun phrase ("the heavy rain"); "although" and "even though" require a clause, and "in spite" needs "of".', 'medium', array['prepositions','conjunctions']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-27', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'She asked me whether I ___ her keys anywhere.', '[{"id":"a","text":"had seen"},{"id":"b","text":"see"},{"id":"c","text":"saw"},{"id":"d","text":"have seen"}]'::jsonb, 'a', 'Reported speech with a past reporting verb ("asked") backshifts the tense: "Have you seen?" becomes "whether I had seen".', 'medium', array['reported-speech','tenses']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-28', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'Not only ___ the cost, but the new system also shortened delivery times.', '[{"id":"a","text":"the new system reduced"},{"id":"b","text":"did the new system reduce"},{"id":"c","text":"the new system did reduce"},{"id":"d","text":"reduced the new system"}]'::jsonb, 'b', '"Not only" at the start of a clause triggers subject–auxiliary inversion: "Not only did the new system reduce...".', 'hard', array['inversion']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-29', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'He suggested ___ a different strategy to solve the problem.', '[{"id":"a","text":"to try"},{"id":"b","text":"try"},{"id":"c","text":"tried"},{"id":"d","text":"trying"}]'::jsonb, 'd', '"Suggest" is followed by a gerund: "suggest doing", never "suggest to do".', 'easy', array['gerunds-infinitives']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-30', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'The more you practice, ___ you become.', '[{"id":"a","text":"the better"},{"id":"b","text":"better"},{"id":"c","text":"the best"},{"id":"d","text":"the more good"}]'::jsonb, 'a', 'Correlative comparatives use "the + comparative, the + comparative": "The more you practice, the better you become".', 'medium', array['comparatives','correlative-conjunctions']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-31', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'By the time you receive this message, the flight ___.', '[{"id":"a","text":"departs"},{"id":"b","text":"will have departed"},{"id":"c","text":"will depart"},{"id":"d","text":"is departing"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'An action completed before a future point uses the future perfect: "will have departed".', 'medium', array['tenses','future-perfect']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-32', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'Neither the report nor the presentation ___ ready in time for the review.', '[{"id":"a","text":"were"},{"id":"b","text":"have been"},{"id":"c","text":"was"},{"id":"d","text":"are"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'With "neither...nor", the verb agrees with the nearer subject — "the presentation" (singular) — so "was" is correct.', 'hard', array['subject-verb-agreement','correlative-conjunctions']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-33', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'By the end of the month, the team ___ the entire training program.', '[{"id":"a","text":"will have completed"},{"id":"b","text":"will complete"},{"id":"c","text":"completes"},{"id":"d","text":"has completed"}]'::jsonb, 'a', 'An action completed before a future point uses the future perfect: "will have completed".', 'medium', array['tenses','future-perfect']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-34', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'The lecture was so long ___ I almost fell asleep.', '[{"id":"a","text":"so"},{"id":"b","text":"that"},{"id":"c","text":"than"},{"id":"d","text":"but"}]'::jsonb, 'b', '"So + adjective + that" introduces a result clause: "so long that I almost fell asleep".', 'easy', array['result-clauses']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-35', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'I wish I ___ harder for the grammar section last week.', '[{"id":"a","text":"study"},{"id":"b","text":"studied"},{"id":"c","text":"had studied"},{"id":"d","text":"have studied"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'A wish about a past action that cannot be changed uses the past perfect: "wish I had studied".', 'medium', array['wishes']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-36', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, '___ his experience, he made several basic mistakes in the report.', '[{"id":"a","text":"Although"},{"id":"b","text":"Even though"},{"id":"c","text":"Because"},{"id":"d","text":"Despite"}]'::jsonb, 'd', '"Despite" takes a noun phrase ("his experience"); "although" and "even though" require a clause.', 'medium', array['prepositions','conjunctions']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-37', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'The manager suggested that the report ___ submitted before the board meeting.', '[{"id":"a","text":"is"},{"id":"b","text":"be"},{"id":"c","text":"was"},{"id":"d","text":"will be"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'After "suggest that", the subjunctive uses the base form of the verb: "be submitted".', 'hard', array['subjunctive']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-38', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'No sooner ___ the exam started than the power went out.', '[{"id":"a","text":"has"},{"id":"b","text":"have"},{"id":"c","text":"had"},{"id":"d","text":"was"}]'::jsonb, 'c', '"No sooner + past perfect + than" describes two immediate past events: "No sooner had the exam started than...".', 'hard', array['inversion','tenses']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-39', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'She is not only a talented writer ___ an excellent speaker.', '[{"id":"a","text":"and"},{"id":"b","text":"or"},{"id":"c","text":"with"},{"id":"d","text":"but also"}]'::jsonb, 'd', '"Not only...but also" is a correlative pair: "not only a talented writer but also an excellent speaker".', 'easy', array['correlative-conjunctions']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-40', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, '___ the fact that it was raining, the game was played as scheduled.', '[{"id":"a","text":"In spite of"},{"id":"b","text":"Even though"},{"id":"c","text":"Because"},{"id":"d","text":"So"}]'::jsonb, 'a', '"In spite of" is a preposition that takes a noun phrase ("the fact that..."), unlike "even though" which needs a clause.', 'medium', array['prepositions','conjunctions']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-41', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'The higher the demand, ___ the prices.', '[{"id":"a","text":"higher"},{"id":"b","text":"the highest"},{"id":"c","text":"the higher"},{"id":"d","text":"the more high"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'Correlative comparatives repeat "the + comparative": "The higher the demand, the higher the prices".', 'medium', array['comparatives','correlative-conjunctions']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-42', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'Had I known about the traffic, I ___ taken the earlier train.', '[{"id":"a","text":"will have"},{"id":"b","text":"would"},{"id":"c","text":"had"},{"id":"d","text":"would have"}]'::jsonb, 'd', '"Had I known" is inverted conditional; the main clause uses "would have + past participle": "I would have taken".', 'hard', array['conditionals','inversion']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-43', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'He objected to ___ his ideas changed without discussion.', '[{"id":"a","text":"having"},{"id":"b","text":"have"},{"id":"c","text":"had"},{"id":"d","text":"to have"}]'::jsonb, 'a', '"Object to" is a verb + preposition combination that takes a gerund: "object to having".', 'hard', array['gerunds-infinitives','prepositions']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-44', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'The results of the experiment ___ published in the journal next month.', '[{"id":"a","text":"will"},{"id":"b","text":"will be"},{"id":"c","text":"are being"},{"id":"d","text":"were"}]'::jsonb, 'b', '"The results" (plural subject) with the passive future: "will be published".', 'medium', array['passive-voice','tenses']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-grammar-45', 'grammar', null, null, null, null, 'Only after she apologized ___ willing to continue the discussion.', '[{"id":"a","text":"she was"},{"id":"b","text":"she is"},{"id":"c","text":"is she"},{"id":"d","text":"was she"}]'::jsonb, 'd', '"Only after..." at the start triggers inversion: "Only after she apologized was she willing...".', 'hard', array['inversion']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-1', 'reading', null, 'passage-water-scarcity', null, null, 'According to the passage, why do traditional solutions like dams offer only temporary relief?', '[{"id":"a","text":"Groundwater reserves take a very long time to naturally refill."},{"id":"b","text":"They are too expensive for most governments to build."},{"id":"c","text":"Governments refuse to pay for new dams in dry regions."},{"id":"d","text":"They only work in coastal cities that have heavy rainfall."}]'::jsonb, 'a', 'The passage states groundwater reserves "take centuries to replenish naturally."', 'easy', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-2', 'reading', null, 'passage-water-scarcity', null, null, 'What is one drawback of desalination mentioned in the passage?', '[{"id":"a","text":"It cannot supply enough water for large cities."},{"id":"b","text":"It consumes a large amount of energy and produces harmful brine."},{"id":"c","text":"It requires drilling much deeper wells than ordinary supplies."},{"id":"d","text":"It only works with fresh water from lakes and rivers."}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'The passage says desalination "consumes enormous amounts of energy" and the leftover brine "can damage marine ecosystems."', 'medium', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-3', 'reading', null, 'passage-water-scarcity', null, null, 'What are researchers currently focusing on, according to the passage?', '[{"id":"a","text":"Banning desalination plants entirely in all countries"},{"id":"b","text":"Building more dams on the largest rivers in the region"},{"id":"c","text":"Making desalination more energy-efficient and encouraging conservation"},{"id":"d","text":"Reducing the price of seawater for desalination plants"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'The passage''s final sentence names energy-efficient desalination, wastewater recycling, and reducing consumption.', 'medium', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-4', 'reading', null, 'passage-remote-work', null, null, 'Before 2020, how was remote work generally perceived?', '[{"id":"a","text":"As a common practice for all employees"},{"id":"b","text":"As illegal in most companies"},{"id":"c","text":"As more productive than office work"},{"id":"d","text":"As a rare privilege mostly for senior employees"}]'::jsonb, 'd', 'The passage states it was "often viewed as a rare privilege offered mainly to senior employees."', 'easy', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-5', 'reading', null, 'passage-remote-work', null, null, 'What challenge did the shift to remote work reveal?', '[{"id":"a","text":"Employees became less skilled at their jobs than before."},{"id":"b","text":"Employees felt isolated and managers struggled to evaluate performance."},{"id":"c","text":"Companies lost most of their clients during the change."},{"id":"d","text":"Office buildings had to be demolished because of the shift."}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'The passage directly mentions isolation and difficulty evaluating performance as challenges.', 'medium', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-6', 'reading', null, 'passage-remote-work', null, null, 'What is a "hybrid model", as described in the passage?', '[{"id":"a","text":"Working fully remotely with no office visits"},{"id":"b","text":"Working only night shifts"},{"id":"c","text":"Coming into the office only a few days a week"},{"id":"d","text":"Hiring only senior employees"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'The passage defines it as requiring employees "to come into the office only a few days a week."', 'easy', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-7', 'reading', null, 'passage-electric-vehicles', null, null, 'According to the passage, what factors contributed to the shift toward electric vehicles?', '[{"id":"a","text":"Higher fuel prices were the only important reason for the shift."},{"id":"b","text":"A ban on combustion engines was already in effect everywhere."},{"id":"c","text":"Improved road infrastructure made electric cars cheaper to run."},{"id":"d","text":"Government incentives, stricter regulations, and falling battery costs"}]'::jsonb, 'd', 'The passage names "government incentives, stricter emissions regulations, and falling battery costs" as contributing factors.', 'easy', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-8', 'reading', null, 'passage-electric-vehicles', null, null, 'What is one obstacle to EV adoption mentioned in the passage?', '[{"id":"a","text":"Limited charging infrastructure outside major cities"},{"id":"b","text":"A lack of consumer interest in electric cars of any kind"},{"id":"c","text":"Low resale value that makes the cars impossible to sell"},{"id":"d","text":"Excessive government taxes charged on every electric car"}]'::jsonb, 'a', 'The passage states "charging infrastructure remains limited in many regions, particularly outside major cities."', 'medium', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-9', 'reading', null, 'passage-electric-vehicles', null, null, 'What are manufacturers currently investing in, according to the passage?', '[{"id":"a","text":"Cheaper gasoline production and larger fuel stations"},{"id":"b","text":"Building more combustion engines for new markets"},{"id":"c","text":"Battery recycling and chemistries that rely less on scarce materials"},{"id":"d","text":"Reducing vehicle safety standards to cut production costs"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'The passage''s final sentence names "battery recycling" and "chemistries that rely less on scarce materials."', 'medium', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-10', 'reading', null, 'passage-multilingual-education', null, null, 'What did some educators fear about early second-language exposure?', '[{"id":"a","text":"It would improve literacy too quickly"},{"id":"b","text":"It would make children bilingual too early"},{"id":"c","text":"It would reduce cognitive flexibility"},{"id":"d","text":"It might slow down native-language literacy development"}]'::jsonb, 'd', 'The passage says some feared early exposure "might slow down literacy development."', 'medium', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-11', 'reading', null, 'passage-multilingual-education', null, null, 'What does recent research suggest about children who learn a second language early?', '[{"id":"a","text":"They show stronger problem-solving skills and cognitive flexibility"},{"id":"b","text":"They struggle with problem-solving and flexible thinking"},{"id":"c","text":"They lose fluency in their native language over time"},{"id":"d","text":"They perform worse academically overall than other children"}]'::jsonb, 'a', 'The passage states such children "often show stronger problem-solving skills and greater cognitive flexibility."', 'medium', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-12', 'reading', null, 'passage-multilingual-education', null, null, 'What does the passage say is required to implement effective multilingual programs?', '[{"id":"a","text":"Reducing curriculum content so lessons are shorter and simpler"},{"id":"b","text":"Well-trained teachers, careful curricula, and sustained investment"},{"id":"c","text":"Eliminating native-language instruction in all subjects"},{"id":"d","text":"Shorter school days and fewer classes each week"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'The passage lists "well-trained teachers, carefully designed curricula, and sustained investment" as requirements.', 'hard', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-13', 'reading', null, 'passage-standardized-testing', null, null, 'According to supporters mentioned in the passage, what is the main advantage of standardized tests?', '[{"id":"a","text":"They are cheaper to administer than any other kind of assessment"},{"id":"b","text":"They remove the need for admissions officers to read applications"},{"id":"c","text":"They are only used by wealthy schools with large budgets"},{"id":"d","text":"They provide an objective, consistent measure for comparing students"}]'::jsonb, 'd', 'The passage says supporters value tests as "an objective, consistent measure that allows fair comparisons."', 'easy', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-14', 'reading', null, 'passage-standardized-testing', null, null, 'What do critics of standardized testing argue, according to the passage?', '[{"id":"a","text":"Tests reward memorization over genuine understanding and disadvantage under-resourced students"},{"id":"b","text":"Tests are too easy for most students, so they measure very little"},{"id":"c","text":"Tests should be mandatory for graduation in every single school"},{"id":"d","text":"Tests improve fairness for all students in every possible case"}]'::jsonb, 'a', 'The passage states critics believe tests reward "test-taking strategy" and can "disadvantage students from under-resourced schools."', 'medium', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-15', 'reading', null, 'passage-standardized-testing', null, null, 'What has been the result of some universities making test scores optional, according to the passage?', '[{"id":"a","text":"All universities have permanently abandoned testing for good"},{"id":"b","text":"Results are mixed — some report more diversity, others find comparisons harder"},{"id":"c","text":"Admissions became simpler and faster for every school"},{"id":"d","text":"Test scores are now required twice for each applicant"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'The passage says early data "has been mixed," citing both more diverse applicant pools and harder comparisons.', 'medium', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-16', 'reading', null, 'passage-vertical-farming', null, null, 'Why do vertical farms not depend on outside weather conditions, according to the passage?', '[{"id":"a","text":"They are built underground, where the weather cannot reach them"},{"id":"b","text":"They only grow crops that do not need any water to survive"},{"id":"c","text":"They use LED lighting and controlled irrigation instead of sunlight and rainfall"},{"id":"d","text":"They import all of their produce from farms in other countries"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'The passage states these farms "use LED lighting and precisely controlled irrigation instead of relying on sunlight and rainfall."', 'easy', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-17', 'reading', null, 'passage-vertical-farming', null, null, 'What benefit related to transportation does the passage mention?', '[{"id":"a","text":"Reduced distance between farm and consumer can lower costs and emissions"},{"id":"b","text":"Farms are always located outside cities, far from the shops"},{"id":"c","text":"Transportation costs have disappeared entirely for farmers"},{"id":"d","text":"Produce must always be shipped internationally to reach shops"}]'::jsonb, 'a', 'The passage credits "reduced transportation distance" with lowering "both costs and carbon emissions."', 'medium', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-18', 'reading', null, 'passage-vertical-farming', null, null, 'What is described as a significant challenge for vertical farming?', '[{"id":"a","text":"A lack of available crops that can grow indoors at all"},{"id":"b","text":"The substantial electricity required for lighting and climate control"},{"id":"c","text":"Difficulty finding enough urban locations for new farms"},{"id":"d","text":"Excessive water usage compared to traditional open-field farms"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'The passage names "the substantial electricity required to power lighting and climate control systems" as the main hurdle.', 'medium', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-19', 'reading', null, 'passage-smart-cities', null, null, 'According to the passage, how do sensors help cities operate more efficiently?', '[{"id":"a","text":"They replace all public transportation with fully automatic vehicles in every district."},{"id":"b","text":"They remove the need for city workers in most departments of the government."},{"id":"c","text":"They allow traffic lights, waste collection, and energy use to be managed based on real-time data."},{"id":"d","text":"They prevent all traffic congestion, even in the busiest districts at rush hour."}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'The passage lists traffic lights adapting to congestion, garbage trucks dispatched when bins are full, and buildings reducing heating as examples of data-driven efficiency.', 'easy', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-20', 'reading', null, 'passage-smart-cities', null, null, 'What concern do critics raise about smart-city systems, according to the passage?', '[{"id":"a","text":"The systems are too expensive for most cities to install."},{"id":"b","text":"The systems are not accurate enough to be of any use."},{"id":"c","text":"The systems cause more traffic than they actually prevent."},{"id":"d","text":"The data collected could be misused to track individuals without consent."}]'::jsonb, 'd', 'Critics warn that detailed movement data "could be misused to track individuals without consent."', 'medium', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-21', 'reading', null, 'passage-smart-cities', null, null, 'How do supporters of smart-city systems respond to the privacy concerns?', '[{"id":"a","text":"They deny that any personal data is collected at all."},{"id":"b","text":"They argue the data is anonymized and protected by strict regulations."},{"id":"c","text":"They admit the concerns are valid and stop the programs."},{"id":"d","text":"They refuse to discuss the issue with the public."}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'Supporters respond that "the data are anonymized and protected by strict regulations," though skeptics remain unconvinced.', 'medium', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-22', 'reading', null, 'passage-smart-cities', null, null, 'What can be inferred from the fact that some cities publish the code and data behind their smart-city programs?', '[{"id":"a","text":"The cities have abandoned their privacy regulations."},{"id":"b","text":"The programs are no longer collecting any data."},{"id":"c","text":"The cities believe public trust is essential for these systems to succeed."},{"id":"d","text":"The cities hope other governments will copy the programs."}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'The passage says cities publish code and data "hoping that transparency will build the public trust that such systems ultimately depend on."', 'hard', array['reading-comprehension','inference']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-23', 'reading', null, 'passage-sleep-academic-performance', null, null, 'According to the passage, what happens during deep sleep?', '[{"id":"a","text":"The brain stops all activity until the next morning begins."},{"id":"b","text":"The body produces large amounts of caffeine to stay alert."},{"id":"c","text":"Memory is erased to make room for new information."},{"id":"d","text":"The brain consolidates the information learned during the day."}]'::jsonb, 'd', 'The passage states that during deep sleep "the brain consolidates the information learned during the day, transferring it from short-term to long-term memory."', 'easy', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-24', 'reading', null, 'passage-sleep-academic-performance', null, null, 'What did studies of university students find, according to the passage?', '[{"id":"a","text":"Students who sleep fewer than seven hours perform worse on recall tasks."},{"id":"b","text":"Students who sleep more than nine hours study less effectively."},{"id":"c","text":"Sleep has no measurable effect on exam grades at all."},{"id":"d","text":"All students need exactly nine hours of sleep to learn well."}]'::jsonb, 'a', 'Studies found that students sleeping "fewer than seven hours a night perform noticeably worse on recall tasks than their well-rested peers."', 'medium', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-25', 'reading', null, 'passage-sleep-academic-performance', null, null, 'What limitation of caffeine does the passage mention?', '[{"id":"a","text":"It is too expensive for most university students to buy."},{"id":"b","text":"It causes permanent damage to the brain after one year."},{"id":"c","text":"It cannot replace the memory consolidation that sleep provides."},{"id":"d","text":"It is banned on most university campuses around the world."}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'Researchers note caffeine "cannot replace the memory consolidation that only sleep provides."', 'medium', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-26', 'reading', null, 'passage-sleep-academic-performance', null, null, 'Which of the following best states the main idea of the passage?', '[{"id":"a","text":"Caffeine is the most reliable way to improve exam performance."},{"id":"b","text":"Universities should assign less homework to all students."},{"id":"c","text":"Studying at night is more effective than studying in the morning."},{"id":"d","text":"Skipping sleep to study more is counterproductive because sleep is needed for memory."}]'::jsonb, 'd', 'The passage argues that sacrificing sleep is "counterproductive" because sleep is required for memory consolidation, which caffeine cannot replace.', 'hard', array['reading-comprehension','main-idea']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-27', 'reading', null, 'passage-ocean-plastic', null, null, 'According to the passage, why can plastic waste persist in the ocean for centuries?', '[{"id":"a","text":"It does not decompose within a practical timeframe."},{"id":"b","text":"It sinks too quickly for organisms to remove it."},{"id":"c","text":"Governments forbid anyone from cleaning it up."},{"id":"d","text":"It only forms in very deep water."}]'::jsonb, 'a', 'The passage says plastic breaks into microplastics that "do not decompose in any practical timeframe."', 'easy', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-28', 'reading', null, 'passage-ocean-plastic', null, null, 'How do microplastics reach humans, according to the passage?', '[{"id":"a","text":"Only by drinking water that comes directly from rivers."},{"id":"b","text":"Through food chains, once marine animals ingest the fragments."},{"id":"c","text":"They cannot reach humans in any way at all."},{"id":"d","text":"Through direct contact with plastic bags on the beach."}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'The passage states ingested microplastics "can travel up the food chain, eventually appearing in the seafood people consume."', 'medium', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-29', 'reading', null, 'passage-ocean-plastic', null, null, 'What do researchers argue about recycling, according to the passage?', '[{"id":"a","text":"Recycling is the only effective solution, and nothing more is needed."},{"id":"b","text":"Recycling is too expensive for any country to attempt on a large scale."},{"id":"c","text":"Recycling has actually made the plastic problem worse in recent years."},{"id":"d","text":"Recycling alone cannot solve the problem because plastic production keeps growing."}]'::jsonb, 'd', 'The passage says recycling alone cannot solve the problem "since plastic production continues to grow faster than waste-management systems can handle it."', 'medium', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-30', 'reading', null, 'passage-ocean-plastic', null, null, 'What does the passage suggest is needed for meaningful progress against ocean plastic?', '[{"id":"a","text":"Reducing plastic production and improving recycling infrastructure"},{"id":"b","text":"Burning plastic waste at sea to reduce its volume"},{"id":"c","text":"Increasing the use of single-use plastic bags in shops"},{"id":"d","text":"Banning all fishing in the world''s oceans"}]'::jsonb, 'a', 'The passage concludes that progress "requires reducing the production of plastic in the first place, alongside better recycling infrastructure and international agreements."', 'medium', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-31', 'reading', null, 'passage-habits', null, null, 'According to the passage, which part of the habit loop is the trigger that starts the behavior?', '[{"id":"a","text":"The reward"},{"id":"b","text":"The cue"},{"id":"c","text":"The routine"},{"id":"d","text":"The willpower"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'The passage defines a cue as "the trigger that starts the behavior."', 'easy', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-32', 'reading', null, 'passage-habits', null, null, 'What does research suggest is more effective than trying to eliminate the cue of a bad habit?', '[{"id":"a","text":"Ignoring the reward entirely and removing the cue instead"},{"id":"b","text":"Punishing yourself after each relapse until the habit stops"},{"id":"c","text":"Replacing the routine while keeping the same cue and reward"},{"id":"d","text":"Studying the habit in great detail for several months"}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'The passage says "replacing an unwanted routine with a healthier one, while keeping the same cue and reward, is far more effective."', 'medium', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-33', 'reading', null, 'passage-habits', null, null, 'Why do people who design their environment find it easier to form new habits, according to the passage?', '[{"id":"a","text":"Their environment makes the desired behavior easy to do."},{"id":"b","text":"They have more willpower than other people."},{"id":"c","text":"Their habits are stored deeper in the brain."},{"id":"d","text":"They avoid all cues completely."}]'::jsonb, 'a', 'The passage says people who "design their surroundings to make the desired behavior easy" are more likely to follow through than those relying on willpower alone.', 'medium', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-34', 'reading', null, 'passage-habits', null, null, 'Which of the following best states the main idea of the passage?', '[{"id":"a","text":"Habits can never be changed once they have been formed."},{"id":"b","text":"Habits are automatic behaviors that can be reshaped through cue, routine, and reward."},{"id":"c","text":"Willpower is the single most important factor in changing behavior."},{"id":"d","text":"Rewards are unnecessary for forming new habits that last."}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'The passage explains the cue-routine-reward loop and how understanding it helps people reshape their habits over time.', 'hard', array['reading-comprehension','main-idea']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-35', 'reading', null, 'passage-public-libraries', null, null, 'How have modern libraries changed beyond lending books, according to the passage?', '[{"id":"a","text":"They now charge fees for every service they offer."},{"id":"b","text":"They stopped offering free access to anyone entirely."},{"id":"c","text":"They act as community hubs offering internet, training, and study space."},{"id":"d","text":"They only serve people who live in wealthy neighborhoods."}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'The passage describes modern libraries as community hubs offering free internet, computer training, job-search assistance, and quiet study space.', 'easy', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-36', 'reading', null, 'passage-public-libraries', null, null, 'Which groups depend on libraries the most, according to the passage?', '[{"id":"a","text":"Tourists who are visiting the city for a short time"},{"id":"b","text":"Employees of large technology companies in the city"},{"id":"c","text":"People who read only on their smartphones every day"},{"id":"d","text":"Residents who cannot afford reliable home internet"}]'::jsonb, 'd', 'The passage says libraries matter most to residents "who cannot afford reliable internet at home."', 'medium', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-37', 'reading', null, 'passage-public-libraries', null, null, 'What does the passage identify as the main threat facing libraries?', '[{"id":"a","text":"Too many books going out of print every year"},{"id":"b","text":"Financial pressure as municipal budgets tighten"},{"id":"c","text":"Competition from smartphone screens and online reading"},{"id":"d","text":"A lack of trained librarians willing to work in cities"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'The passage states the main threat is financial: "as municipal budgets tighten, branches are sometimes closed or their hours reduced."', 'medium', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-38', 'reading', null, 'passage-public-libraries', null, null, 'Which statement best reflects the author''s view of public libraries?', '[{"id":"a","text":"Libraries are outdated and should be closed to save public money."},{"id":"b","text":"Libraries should focus only on lending printed books to local readers."},{"id":"c","text":"Libraries remain valuable as free, trusted providers of information and services."},{"id":"d","text":"Libraries will soon be replaced by private companies that charge for access."}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'Supporters (with whom the passage aligns) argue "the library''s role as a trusted, free provider of reliable information is more valuable than ever."', 'hard', array['reading-comprehension','inference']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-39', 'reading', null, 'passage-urban-green-spaces', null, null, 'According to the passage, how have city planners traditionally viewed parks?', '[{"id":"a","text":"As essential infrastructure, on par with roads and bridges"},{"id":"b","text":"As important sources of tax revenue for the city"},{"id":"c","text":"As the best solution to housing shortages in cities"},{"id":"d","text":"As mainly decorative additions to the urban landscape"}]'::jsonb, 'd', 'The passage opens by saying planners "have long viewed parks and gardens mainly as decorative additions."', 'easy', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-40', 'reading', null, 'passage-urban-green-spaces', null, null, 'What is one explanation the passage gives for why green spaces improve health?', '[{"id":"a","text":"Time in nature lowers stress hormones and encourages physical activity."},{"id":"b","text":"They attract new residents who bring more business to the area."},{"id":"c","text":"They raise property prices and local tax income for the city."},{"id":"d","text":"They replace the need for hospitals in the neighborhood."}]'::jsonb, 'a', 'The passage cites lower cortisol levels and increased walking and cycling as the health link.', 'medium', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-41', 'reading', null, 'passage-urban-green-spaces', null, null, 'Why does the passage say low-income neighborhoods often have the least access to parks?', '[{"id":"a","text":"Residents there prefer private gardens."},{"id":"b","text":"Government regulations ban new parks in those areas."},{"id":"c","text":"Land there is cheaper to develop for housing or industry."},{"id":"d","text":"Residents have no interest in outdoor activities."}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'The passage says land in these neighborhoods is often "cheaper to develop for housing or industry."', 'medium', array['reading-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-reading-42', 'reading', null, 'passage-urban-green-spaces', null, null, 'Which caution does the author raise at the end of the passage?', '[{"id":"a","text":"Cities should stop planting trees along their main boulevards."},{"id":"b","text":"Green spaces actually cause more cardiovascular disease."},{"id":"c","text":"Pocket parks are too expensive for most cities to build."},{"id":"d","text":"Counting parks alone is not enough because quality and safety determine actual use."}]'::jsonb, 'd', 'The author warns that "the quality and safety of a space determine whether residents actually use it."', 'hard', array['reading-comprehension','inference']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-listening-1', 'listening', null, null, '/audio/listening/q-listening-1.mp3', 'Man: Excuse me, does this bus go to the central library?
Woman: No, you need the number 12. This one goes to the airport.
Man: Oh, I see. Where can I catch the number 12?
Woman: Just across the street, at the stop next to the pharmacy.', 'Where does the woman tell the man to catch the number 12 bus?', '[{"id":"a","text":"Across the street, next to the pharmacy"},{"id":"b","text":"At the airport, near the main departures hall"},{"id":"c","text":"At the central library, in front of the main entrance"},{"id":"d","text":"At the same stop where he is standing now"}]'::jsonb, 'a', 'The woman says the stop is "across the street, at the stop next to the pharmacy."', 'easy', array['listening-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-listening-2', 'listening', null, null, '/audio/listening/q-listening-1.mp3', 'Man: Excuse me, does this bus go to the central library?
Woman: No, you need the number 12. This one goes to the airport.
Man: Oh, I see. Where can I catch the number 12?
Woman: Just across the street, at the stop next to the pharmacy.', 'What is this bus''s actual destination?', '[{"id":"a","text":"The central library"},{"id":"b","text":"The airport"},{"id":"c","text":"The pharmacy"},{"id":"d","text":"It has no destination mentioned"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'The woman says, "This one goes to the airport."', 'easy', array['listening-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-listening-3', 'listening', null, null, '/audio/listening/q-listening-1.mp3', 'Man: Excuse me, does this bus go to the central library?
Woman: No, you need the number 12. This one goes to the airport.
Man: Oh, I see. Where can I catch the number 12?
Woman: Just across the street, at the stop next to the pharmacy.', 'What can be inferred about the man at the start of the conversation?', '[{"id":"a","text":"He works at the pharmacy."},{"id":"b","text":"He is the bus driver."},{"id":"c","text":"He is unsure which bus to take."},{"id":"d","text":"He has taken this bus many times before."}]'::jsonb, 'c', 'He asks whether the bus goes to the library, showing he isn''t sure he''s on the right one.', 'medium', array['listening-inference']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-listening-4', 'listening', null, null, '/audio/listening/q-listening-4.mp3', 'Woman: Did you finish the quarterly report?
Man: Almost. I just need the sales figures from Ahmed.
Woman: He''s out sick today, unfortunately.
Man: Then I''ll use last month''s figures as an estimate and update it tomorrow.', 'Why hasn''t the man finished the report?', '[{"id":"a","text":"He does not know how to write reports of this kind."},{"id":"b","text":"The report was already submitted to the manager yesterday."},{"id":"c","text":"The woman has not yet approved the first draft."},{"id":"d","text":"He is waiting for figures from a colleague who is absent."}]'::jsonb, 'd', 'He says he needs sales figures from Ahmed, who "is out sick today."', 'medium', array['listening-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
insert into public.questions (id, section, course_id, passage_id, audio_url, transcript, prompt, options, correct_option_id, explanation, difficulty, tags) values ('q-listening-5', 'listening', null, null, '/audio/listening/q-listening-4.mp3', 'Woman: Did you finish the quarterly report?
Man: Almost. I just need the sales figures from Ahmed.
Woman: He''s out sick today, unfortunately.
Man: Then I''ll use last month''s figures as an estimate and update it tomorrow.', 'What does the man decide to do?', '[{"id":"a","text":"Cancel the report and tell the manager why"},{"id":"b","text":"Use an estimate now and update it later"},{"id":"c","text":"Wait until Ahmed returns before he does anything"},{"id":"d","text":"Ask the woman to write the report instead of him"}]'::jsonb, 'b', 'He says, "I''ll use last month''s figures as an estimate and update it tomorrow."', 'medium', array['listening-comprehension']::text[]) on conflict (id) do update set section = excluded.section, course_id = excluded.course_id, passage_id = excluded.passage_id, audio_url = excluded.audio_url, transcript = excluded.transcript, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options, correct_option_id = excluded.correct_option_id, explanation = excluded.explanation, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, tags = excluded.tags;
